window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-ncert11-bio-mineral-nutrition","kind":"practice","exam":"NEET","year":null,"title":"Mineral Nutrition · Practice","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","subject":"Biology","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-001","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":1,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following is a beneficial element, rather than an essential element, for higher plants?","image":"","options":["Nickel","Chlorine","Selenium","Iron"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT lists sodium, silicon, cobalt and selenium as beneficial elements. Higher plants require them, but they do not fulfil all the criteria of essentiality.\nNickel, chlorine and iron are all essential micronutrients.\nNickel may look unusual, but it is counted among the 17 essential elements.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-002","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":2,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Deficiency symptoms of an element tend to appear first in young leaves. It indicates that the element is relatively immobile. Which one of the following elemental deficiency would show such symptoms?","image":"","options":["sulphur","magnesium","nitrogen","potassium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Mobile elements are moved from older to younger tissues, so their deficiency shows first in older leaves.\nNitrogen, potassium and magnesium are mobile, so their symptoms appear first in older (senescent) leaves.\nSulphur and calcium are relatively immobile because they are part of structural components and are not readily released.\nTheir deficiency therefore shows first in young leaves.\nOf the options given, only sulphur fits.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 12 (MCQ Q3)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-003","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":3,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (element) with List-II (role described in NCERT):\nList-I\nA. Magnesium\nB. Zinc\nC. Molybdenum\nD. Potassium\nList-II\n(i) Activator of alcohol dehydrogenase\n(ii) Activator of nitrogenase during nitrogen metabolism\n(iii) Alters osmotic potential and helps in opening and closing of stomata\n(iv) Activator of RuBisCO and PEPcase\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-(iv), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iii)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(iii)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-(iv): Mg$^{2+}$ activates RuBisCO and PEPcase, the enzymes of photosynthetic carbon fixation.\nB-(i): Zn$^{2+}$ activates alcohol dehydrogenase.\nC-(ii): Mo activates nitrogenase during nitrogen metabolism.\nD-(iii): K changes the osmotic potential of cells and so controls the opening and closing of stomata.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-004","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":4,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"The amide asparagine is formed in plants from which amino acid?","image":"","options":["Glutamic acid","Aspartic acid","Alanine","Glycine"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Plants form two important amides by adding a second amino group to an amino acid.\nAsparagine is formed from aspartic acid.\nGlutamine is formed from glutamic acid. This is why glutamic acid is the likely confusion, since it gives glutamine, not asparagine.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-005","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":5,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements about the functions of essential elements is incorrect?","image":"","options":["$Mg^{2+}$ activates both RuBisCO and PEPcase, enzymes of photosynthetic carbon fixation","Molybdenum is an activator of nitrogenase during nitrogen metabolism","Phosphorus is the element that forms part of chlorophyll, making it a component of an energy-related compound","Magnesium belongs to more than one functional category of essential elements"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: $Mg^{2+}$ activates RuBisCO and PEPcase.\n(2) Correct: Mo activates nitrogenase in nitrogen metabolism.\n(3) Incorrect: Mg is the element in chlorophyll. Phosphorus is NCERT's example of an energy-related element because it is part of ATP.\n(4) Correct: Mg is both a component of chlorophyll and an enzyme activator, so it falls in two categories.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-006","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":6,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about biological nitrogen fixation in legume root nodules is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Nitrogenase is a Mo-Fe protein that catalyses the conversion of atmospheric N$_2$ to NH$_3$","Leghaemoglobin acts as an oxygen scavenger and protects nitrogenase","The nodule contains all the necessary biochemical components, such as nitrogenase and leghaemoglobin","Reduction of one molecule of N$_2$ to two molecules of NH$_3$ uses 8 ATP"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: nitrogenase is a Mo-Fe protein that reduces N$_2$ to ammonia.\n(2) Correct: nitrogenase is sensitive to O$_2$, and the pink leghaemoglobin scavenges oxygen.\n(3) Correct: NCERT states that nodules have all these components.\n(4) Incorrect: the overall reaction is N$_2$ + 8e$^-$ + 8H$^+$ + 16ATP → 2NH$_3$ + H$_2$ + 16ADP + 16Pi. It uses 16 ATP, not 8. Only the electrons and protons number 8.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-007","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":7,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (event or component in nodule formation) with List-II (its role):\nList-I\nA. Curling of root hair\nB. Infection thread\nC. Division of inner cortical and pericycle cells\nD. Vascular connection of mature nodule\nList-II\n(i) Builds the body of the nodule\n(ii) Exchange of nutrients between nodule and host\n(iii) Indicates successful infection of the root hair\n(iv) Conveys bacteria to the inner cortex","image":"","options":["A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A-(iii): Successful infection of the root hair causes it to curl.\nB-(iv): The infection thread carries the bacteria to the inner cortex.\nC-(i): Division and growth of cortical and pericycle cells form the nodule.\nD-(ii): The vascular tissue of the mature nodule is continuous with the root and allows exchange of nutrients.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-008","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":8,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"The following events (not in order) occur during root nodule formation in a legume:\n(i) An infection thread carries the bacteria into the root cortex\n(ii) Rhizobia multiply around the root and attach to epidermal and root hair cells\n(iii) Bacteria are released from the thread into cells and differentiate into nitrogen-fixing bacteroids\n(iv) Root hairs curl and are invaded by the bacteria\n(v) Nodule formation is initiated in the cortex\nWhich option gives the correct sequence?","image":"","options":["(ii) → (i) → (iv) → (v) → (iii)","(ii) → (iv) → (i) → (v) → (iii)","(iv) → (ii) → (i) → (iii) → (v)","(ii) → (iv) → (v) → (i) → (iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Step 1 (ii): Rhizobia multiply, colonise the area around the root and attach to epidermal and root hair cells.\nStep 2 (iv): The root hairs curl and the bacteria invade them.\nStep 3 (i): An infection thread forms and carries the bacteria into the cortex.\nStep 4 (v): Nodule formation is initiated in the cortex.\nStep 5 (iii): The bacteria are released into the cells and become bacteroids. The nodule then forms a vascular connection with the host.\nOption (4) is wrong because the nodule starts only after the infection thread reaches the cortex.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-009","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":9,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following contain sulphur?\nCysteine, methionine, thiamine, biotin, ferredoxin, lysine, coenzyme A, riboflavin","image":"","options":["4","5","6","7"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Sulphur is present in two amino acids, cysteine and methionine.\nIt is also a constituent of the vitamins thiamine and biotin, of coenzyme A and of ferredoxin. That makes six sulphur-containing compounds.\nLysine is an amino acid with no sulphur.\nRiboflavin (vitamin B2) also contains no sulphur.\nTotal = 6.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-010","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":10,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"In the nitrogen cycle, bacteria such as Pseudomonas and Thiobacillus carry out a step that returns nitrogen to the atmosphere. Which conversion do they bring about?","image":"","options":["Ammonia to nitrite","Nitrite to nitrate","Nitrate to free nitrogen ($N_2$)","Free nitrogen ($N_2$) to ammonia"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Pseudomonas and Thiobacillus are denitrifying bacteria.\nDenitrification reduces nitrate present in the soil to free nitrogen gas, which goes back to the atmosphere.\nAmmonia to nitrite is done by Nitrosomonas or Nitrococcus, and nitrite to nitrate by Nitrobacter. Together these two steps are nitrification.\nThe conversion of $N_2$ to ammonia is nitrogen fixation, carried out by organisms that have nitrogenase.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-011","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":11,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The same deficiency symptom in a plant may be caused by the deficiency of any one of several different elements.\nStatement II: To identify the deficient element, it is sufficient to examine the symptoms on the youngest leaves alone.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. For example, chlorosis can arise from lack of N, Fe, Mg or several other elements.\nStatement II is incorrect. NCERT says all symptoms in all parts of the plant must be studied and compared with standard tables.\nYoung leaves alone would miss symptoms of mobile elements, which appear first in older leaves.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-012","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":12,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Deficiency symptoms of nitrogen are first visible in the senescent leaves of a plant.\nReason (R): In older leaves, biomolecules containing nitrogen are broken down, and the nitrogen released is mobilised to younger leaves.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: nitrogen is actively mobilised, so its deficiency shows first in older leaves.\nR is true: NCERT explains that nitrogen-containing biomolecules in older leaves are broken down and the element is exported to young developing tissues.\nThis export is exactly why the old leaves become deficient first, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-013","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":13,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I (Element)\nA. Calcium\nB. Phosphorus\nC. Sulphur\nD. Potassium\nList-II (Role)\n(i) Constituent of ferredoxin and coenzyme A\n(ii) Needed for formation of the mitotic spindle\n(iii) Required for all phosphorylation reactions\n(iv) Involved in opening and closing of stomata","image":"","options":["A-iii, B-ii, C-iv, D-i","A-ii, B-iii, C-i, D-iv","A-ii, B-i, C-iii, D-iv","A-iv, B-iii, C-i, D-ii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A–(ii): Calcium is needed when the mitotic spindle forms.\nB–(iii): Phosphorus is required for all phosphorylation reactions.\nC–(i): Sulphur is a part of ferredoxin and coenzyme A.\nD–(iv): Potassium is involved in the opening and closing of stomata.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-014","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":14,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following elements are micronutrients for plants?\nIron, Manganese, Calcium, Molybdenum, Sulphur, Boron, Nickel, Magnesium","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The micronutrients in this list are iron, manganese, molybdenum, boron and nickel, which makes 5.\nIron is a micronutrient even though plants need more of it than of other micronutrients.\nCalcium, sulphur and magnesium are macronutrients.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-015","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":15,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Match the element in List-I with its role in List-II.\nList-I:\nA. Calcium\nB. Potassium\nC. Magnesium\nD. Iron\nList-II:\n(i) Maintains the structure of ribosomes\n(ii) Activates the enzyme catalase\n(iii) Used in synthesis of the middle lamella\n(iv) Helps in opening and closing of stomata","image":"","options":["A-(i), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A. Calcium forms calcium pectate, which is used to make the middle lamella: A-(iii).\nB. Potassium helps open and close stomata and maintains turgidity: B-(iv).\nC. Magnesium maintains ribosome structure and is part of the chlorophyll ring: C-(i).\nD. Iron activates catalase and is a component of ferredoxin and cytochromes: D-(ii).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-016","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":16,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"A crop plant shows yellowing that begins in its young leaves rather than its old ones. It also shows inhibited cell division and delayed flowering. Deficiency of which element best explains all these observations?","image":"","options":["Sulphur","Nitrogen","Calcium","Magnesium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Sulphur satisfies every clue. It causes chlorosis, inhibits cell division and delays flowering, and it is relatively immobile, so symptoms start in young leaves.\nNitrogen matches the chlorosis, cell-division and flowering clues, but it is mobile, so its symptoms appear first in old leaves.\nCalcium is immobile but does not cause chlorosis.\nMagnesium is mobile and is not linked to delayed flowering.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-017","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":17,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"In a solution-culture experiment, plants are grown in a complete nutrient solution and then moved to a solution lacking one element, X. Yellowing appears first in the older, lower leaves, while the young leaves stay green for longer. Which conclusion is most appropriate?","image":"","options":["X is relatively immobile, like calcium, and stays in older tissues","X is mobile, like nitrogen, and is moved from older leaves to younger ones","X is not an essential element because young leaves remain healthy","X must be a beneficial element such as sodium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Solution-culture experiments that leave out one element reveal its deficiency symptoms.\nIf symptoms show first in older leaves, the element is being moved out of those leaves to the young, growing parts. This means it is mobile, as nitrogen, potassium and magnesium are.\nImmobile elements such as calcium and sulphur show symptoms first in young leaves.\nThe plant still shows deficiency, so X is clearly needed. Options (3) and (4) are therefore wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-018","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":18,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Ammonium ions are accumulated in large amounts in plant cells as a reserve form of nitrogen.\nStatement II: Glutamic acid is the main amino acid from which the amino group is transferred to form other amino acids by transamination.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. $NH_4^+$ is quite toxic to plants and cannot accumulate. It is quickly used up to make amino acids.\nStatement II is correct. Glutamic acid is the chief donor of the $-NH_2$ group in transamination reactions.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-019","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":19,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"In a hydroponic experiment, plants grown without element X fail to complete their life cycle and do not set seeds. X is also directly involved in their metabolism. However, when element Y is supplied in place of X, the plants grow normally and set seeds. What can be concluded about X?","image":"","options":["X is essential because its absence prevents seed setting","X is not essential because its requirement is not specific and can be met by Y","X is essential because it is directly involved in metabolism","X must be a beneficial element such as sodium or selenium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"An essential element must satisfy all three criteria: it is absolutely necessary for growth and reproduction, its requirement is specific (no other element can replace it), and it is directly involved in metabolism.\nX meets the first and third criteria. It fails the second, because Y replaces it.\nOptions (1) and (3) each rely on only one criterion, so they are wrong.\nNothing in the data shows X is a beneficial element, so (4) is wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-020","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":20,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are correct about reductive amination in plants?\n(i) NADPH acts as the reducing agent\n(ii) α-ketoglutaric acid is the acceptor of ammonium\n(iii) Glutamate is the amino acid formed\n(iv) Water is one of the products\n(v) The reaction is catalysed by transaminase\n(vi) Oxaloacetic acid is the keto acid that accepts ammonium","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The reaction is α-ketoglutaric acid + $NH_4^+$ + NADPH → glutamate + $H_2O$ + $NADP^+$.\n(i) Correct: NADPH supplies the reducing power.\n(ii) Correct: α-ketoglutaric acid accepts the ammonium.\n(iii) Correct: glutamate is formed.\n(iv) Correct: water is released.\n(v) Incorrect: the enzyme is glutamate dehydrogenase, not transaminase.\n(vi) Incorrect: the acceptor is α-ketoglutaric acid, not oxaloacetic acid.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-021","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":21,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Iron is a constituent of ferredoxin and cytochromes, which transfer electrons.\nReason (R): During electron transfer, iron is reversibly oxidised from $Fe^{2+}$ to $Fe^{3+}$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: iron is part of electron-transfer proteins such as ferredoxin and cytochromes.\nR is true: iron changes reversibly between $Fe^{2+}$ and $Fe^{3+}$ during electron transfer.\nThis ability to gain and lose an electron is exactly why iron suits electron carriers, so R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-022","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":22,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements about mineral deficiency in plants is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Deficiency symptoms usually disappear when the deficient nutrient is supplied, but prolonged deprivation can kill the plant","Different plant species may respond differently to the deficiency of the same element","Low concentration of potassium in the plant delays flowering, as with nitrogen and sulphur","An element is said to be deficient when it is present below its critical concentration"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: symptoms vanish once the nutrient is provided, but continued deprivation may cause death.\n(2) Correct: NCERT notes that different plants respond differently to the same deficiency.\n(3) Incorrect: NCERT names only N, S and Mo as delaying flowering. Potassium deficiency causes chlorosis, necrosis and inhibited cell division, not delayed flowering.\n(4) Correct: this is NCERT's definition of a deficient element.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-023","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":23,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Plants deficient in which element show reduced synthesis of auxin and lower activity of carboxylase enzymes?","image":"","options":["Boron","Molybdenum","Zinc","Chlorine"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Zinc ($Zn^{2+}$) activates various enzymes, especially carboxylases, and is needed for auxin synthesis.\nIts deficiency therefore lowers both auxin production and carboxylase activity.\nBoron affects pollen germination and cell elongation, not auxin synthesis.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-024","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":24,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"In which ionic form do plants absorb sulphur from the soil?","image":"","options":["$SO_3^{2-}$","$SO_4^{2-}$","$S^{2-}$","$H_2S$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Sulphur is a macronutrient that plants take up from the soil solution as the sulphate ion, $SO_4^{2-}$.\nSulphite ($SO_3^{2-}$) and sulphide ($S^{2-}$) are not the forms NCERT gives for uptake.\n$H_2S$ is a gas produced by some microbes. Roots do not absorb it as a nutrient.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-025","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":25,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"In a root nodule, the energy required for nitrogen fixation is 8 ATP for each $NH_3$ produced. How many ATP are consumed to reduce one molecule of $N_2$ completely to ammonia?","image":"","options":["8","16","24","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Nitrogenase converts one $N_2$ into two molecules of $NH_3$.\nEach $NH_3$ needs 8 ATP, so one $N_2$ needs $2 \\times 8$ = 16 ATP.\nThis ATP comes from respiration of the host cells.\nOption 8 is tempting, but that is the cost per $NH_3$, not per $N_2$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-026","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":26,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Nitrate absorbed by plants is transported to the leaves, where it is reduced to ammonia that finally forms the amine group of amino acids.\nStatement II: Most of the ammonia released by ammonification volatilises and is lost to the atmosphere, so very little is converted to nitrate.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT says that nitrate taken up by plants reaches the leaves, is reduced to ammonia and is built into the amine group of amino acids.\nStatement II is incorrect. Only some ammonia volatilises and re-enters the atmosphere. Most of it is converted to nitrate by soil bacteria through nitrification.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-027","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":27,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (element) with List-II (role in plants).\nList-I:\nA. Calcium\nB. Potassium\nC. Sulphur\nD. Magnesium\nList-II:\ni. Helps in the opening and closing of stomata\nii. Constituent of coenzyme A and ferredoxin\niii. Used in the synthesis of the middle lamella and the formation of the mitotic spindle\niv. Maintains the structure of ribosomes","image":"","options":["A-iii, B-i, C-ii, D-iv","A-iv, B-i, C-ii, D-iii","A-iii, B-ii, C-i, D-iv","A-i, B-iii, C-iv, D-ii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-iii: Calcium forms calcium pectate of the middle lamella and is needed for the mitotic spindle.\nB-i: Potassium regulates the opening and closing of stomata.\nC-ii: Sulphur is a constituent of coenzyme A, ferredoxin and some vitamins.\nD-iv: Magnesium maintains ribosome structure, besides forming part of the chlorophyll ring.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-028","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":28,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about boron in plants is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["It is required for the uptake and utilisation of $Ca^{2+}$","It is needed for pollen germination and cell elongation","It plays a role in carbohydrate translocation","It activates carboxylases and is needed for auxin synthesis"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: boron is required for uptake and utilisation of $Ca^{2+}$ and for membrane functioning.\n(2) Correct: boron is needed for pollen germination, cell elongation and cell differentiation.\n(3) Correct: boron helps in carbohydrate translocation.\n(4) Incorrect: activating carboxylases and auxin synthesis are functions of zinc.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-029","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":29,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (agent) with List-II (conversion it brings about) and choose the correct option.\nList-I\nA. Lightning\nB. Nitrosomonas\nC. Nitrobacter\nD. Thiobacillus\nList-II\n(i) $NO_2^-$ to $NO_3^-$\n(ii) $N_2$ to nitrogen oxides\n(iii) $NO_3^-$ to $N_2$\n(iv) $NH_3$ to $NO_2^-$","image":"","options":["A-ii, B-iv, C-i, D-iii","A-ii, B-i, C-iv, D-iii","A-iii, B-iv, C-i, D-ii","A-iv, B-ii, C-iii, D-i"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-ii: The energy of lightning converts $N_2$ into nitrogen oxides.\nB-iv: Nitrosomonas (and Nitrococcus) oxidise ammonia to nitrite, which is the first step of nitrification.\nC-i: Nitrobacter oxidises nitrite to nitrate, which is the second step of nitrification.\nD-iii: Thiobacillus, like Pseudomonas, carries out denitrification and reduces soil nitrate to $N_2$.\nOption (2) wrongly swaps the two nitrifying bacteria.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-030","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":30,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"In some plants such as soybean, the root nodules export fixed nitrogen to other parts of the plant through the transpiration stream mainly in the form of:","image":"","options":["Nitrates","Free ammonium ions","Ureides","Proteins"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the nodules of some plants, for example soybean, export fixed nitrogen as ureides.\nUreides travel in the xylem along with the transpiration stream and have a high N:C ratio.\nFree ammonium cannot be transported this way because it is toxic and cannot accumulate.\nProteins are not carried in the xylem.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-031","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":31,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Magnesium is a component of an energy-related compound in plants and also acts as an enzyme activator.\nStatement II: Macronutrients are generally present in plant tissues in amounts less than 10 mmole per kg of dry matter.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Mg is part of chlorophyll, an energy-related compound, and Mg$^{2+}$ activates RuBisCO and PEPcase. One element can therefore fall in more than one functional category.\nStatement II is incorrect. Macronutrients are present in excess of 10 mmole per kg of dry matter. Less than 10 mmole per kg describes micronutrients.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-032","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":32,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Plants growing in soil with an excess of manganese may show symptoms of magnesium deficiency.\nReason (R): Manganese competes with iron and magnesium for uptake, and with magnesium for binding with enzymes.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT notes that the apparent deficiency symptoms of Fe, Mg and Ca may actually be caused by manganese toxicity.\nR is true. Excess Mn competes with Fe and Mg for uptake and with Mg for binding to enzymes. It also inhibits Ca translocation to the shoot apex.\nThis competition lowers the Mg available to the plant, so R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-033","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":33,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about biological nitrogen fixation in legume root nodules are correct?\n(i) Nitrogenase is a Mo-Fe protein.\n(ii) Ammonia is the first stable product of nitrogen fixation.\n(iii) About 8 ATP are used for each $NH_3$ produced.\n(iv) The energy for fixation comes mainly from photosynthesis carried out by the bacteroids.\n(v) Hydrogen gas is also released in the nitrogenase reaction.\n(vi) Leghaemoglobin supplies oxygen to nitrogenase to activate it.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: nitrogenase is a Mo-Fe protein.\n(ii) Correct: ammonia is the first stable product.\n(iii) Correct: $N_2 + 8e^- + 8H^+ + 16ATP \\rightarrow 2NH_3 + H_2 + 16ADP + 16P_i$ gives 8 ATP per $NH_3$.\n(iv) Incorrect: bacteroids are not photosynthetic. The energy comes from respiration of the host cells.\n(v) Correct: $H_2$ is a product of the reaction.\n(vi) Incorrect: leghaemoglobin removes oxygen, because oxygen inactivates nitrogenase.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-034","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":34,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following roles is not characteristic of an essential element?","image":"","options":["being a component of biomolecules","changing the chemistry of soil","being a structural component of energy related chemical compounds","activation or inhibition of enzymes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Inside the plant, essential elements act as components of biomolecules such as carbohydrates, proteins and nucleic acids.\nThey are part of energy-related compounds, for example Mg in chlorophyll and P in ATP.\nThey activate or inhibit enzymes, for example Mg$^{2+}$ activates RuBisCO.\nThey also change the osmotic potential of cells, for example K$^+$ in stomatal opening.\nChanging soil chemistry is not a role they play within the plant, so (2) is not characteristic.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 12 (MCQ Q1)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-035","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":35,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"The movement of ions across a membrane is called flux. What is the inward movement of ions into the cell called?","image":"","options":["Efflux","Diffusion pressure","Influx","Exosmosis"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT calls ion movement 'flux'.\nInward movement of ions into cells is influx.\nOutward movement of ions is efflux.\nExosmosis refers to water moving out of a cell, not to ions.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-036","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":36,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"The following events lead to root nodule formation in a legume:\nP. An infection thread carries the bacteria into the root cortex\nQ. Rhizobia multiply, colonise the area around the roots and attach to epidermal and root hair cells\nR. Root hairs curl\nS. Bacteria are released from the thread into cells, which differentiate into specialised nitrogen-fixing cells\nT. Nodule formation is initiated in the cortex\nChoose the correct sequence.","image":"","options":["Q, P, R, T, S","Q, R, P, T, S","R, Q, P, S, T","Q, R, T, P, S"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Step 1: Rhizobia multiply near the root and attach to epidermal and root hair cells (Q).\nStep 2: Root hairs curl around the bacteria (R).\nStep 3: An infection thread carries the bacteria into the cortex (P).\nStep 4: Nodule formation begins in the cortex (T).\nStep 5: Bacteria are released into cells, which become nitrogen-fixing cells (S).\nThe order is Q, R, P, T, S. Option (4) wrongly starts nodule formation before the bacteria reach the cortex.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-037","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":37,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (micronutrient) with List-II (role):\nList-I\n(A) Zinc\n(B) Boron\n(C) Chlorine\n(D) Copper\nList-II\n(i) Determines solute concentration and anion-cation balance along with $Na^+$ and $K^+$\n(ii) Associated with enzymes of redox reactions; reversibly oxidised from $Cu^{+}$ to $Cu^{2+}$\n(iii) Required for synthesis of auxin\n(iv) Required for pollen germination and carbohydrate translocation","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Zn: activates carboxylases and is needed for auxin synthesis, so A-(iii).\nB: needed for $Ca^{2+}$ uptake, pollen germination, cell elongation and carbohydrate translocation, so B-(iv).\nCl: works with $Na^+$ and $K^+$ to maintain solute concentration and anion-cation balance, so C-(i).\nCu: part of redox enzymes and cycles between $Cu^+$ and $Cu^{2+}$, so D-(ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-038","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":38,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"In a root nodule, nitrogenase reduces $N_2$ according to NCERT's equation: $N_2 + 8e^- + 8H^+ + 16ATP \\rightarrow 2NH_3 + H_2 + 16ADP + 16P_i$. How many ATP molecules are consumed to produce 6 molecules of ammonia?","image":"","options":["24","48","16","96"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: one $N_2$ uses 16 ATP and yields 2 $NH_3$.\nFormula: ATP needed = (number of $NH_3$ / 2) × 16, which is 8 ATP per $NH_3$.\nSubstituting: 6 $NH_3$ need 3 $N_2$, so 3 × 16 = 48 ATP.\nSo the ATP consumed is 48. Option 24 wrongly counts only 4 ATP per $NH_3$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-039","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":39,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following plants can form nitrogen-fixing root nodules in symbiosis with Rhizobium?\nAlfalfa, Sweet clover, Alnus, Lentil, Broad bean, Maize","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Rhizobium forms nodules on the roots of legumes.\nAlfalfa: yes, it is a legume.\nSweet clover: yes, it is a legume.\nLentil: yes, it is a legume.\nBroad bean: yes, it is a legume.\nAlnus: no. It is a non-leguminous plant, and its nodules are formed by Frankia.\nMaize: no. It is a cereal and does not form Rhizobium nodules.\nSo 4 of the six plants qualify.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-040","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":40,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is the prominent visible symptom of manganese toxicity in plants?","image":"","options":["Yellowing of the whole leaf blade starting from the tips of old leaves","Purple colouration of stems and petioles","Brown spots surrounded by chlorotic veins","Rosette formation and little leaf"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT describes the prominent symptom of manganese toxicity as brown spots surrounded by chlorotic veins.\nYellowing that begins in older leaves is typical of nitrogen deficiency.\nLittle leaf and rosette formation are linked with zinc deficiency, not with excess manganese.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-041","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":41,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Absorbed mineral salts are carried from the roots to the aerial parts of a plant mainly through:","image":"","options":["Phloem, driven by source–sink pressure flow","Xylem, along with the ascending stream of water pulled by transpiration","Xylem, driven only by root pressure generated by ATP","Cortical cells, by cell-to-cell active transport throughout the stem"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Mineral salts travel in the xylem together with the upward stream of water.\nThis water is pulled up through the plant by transpirational pull.\nPhloem pressure flow carries organic food, not the bulk of absorbed minerals, so option (1) is wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-042","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":42,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"In plant cells at physiological pH, ammonia mostly exists in which form?","image":"","options":["Nitrite ion ($NO_2^-$)","Ammonium ion ($NH_4^+$)","Free gaseous $NH_3$","Nitrate ion ($NO_3^-$)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"At physiological pH, ammonia picks up a proton and becomes the ammonium ion, $NH_4^+$.\nNitrite and nitrate are oxidised forms produced by nitrifying bacteria. They are not formed by protonation.\nFree $NH_3$ does not remain unprotonated at this pH.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-043","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":43,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Nitrogen is required by all parts of a plant, particularly by meristematic tissues and metabolically active cells.\nReason (R): Nitrogen is a major constituent of proteins, nucleic acids, vitamins and hormones.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that nitrogen is needed by all parts of the plant, especially meristems and metabolically active cells.\nR is true. Nitrogen is a major constituent of proteins, nucleic acids, vitamins and hormones.\nDividing and active cells make large amounts of proteins and nucleic acids. So R correctly explains why these cells need so much nitrogen.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-044","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":44,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following roles are attributed to iron in plants?\n(a) Constituent of ferredoxin\n(b) Constituent of cytochromes involved in electron transfer\n(c) Activation of the enzyme catalase\n(d) Essential for the formation of chlorophyll\n(e) Required for the synthesis of auxin\n(f) Required for the uptake and utilisation of $Ca^{2+}$\n(g) Component of nitrate reductase","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) Correct: iron is a constituent of ferredoxin.\n(b) Correct: iron is a constituent of cytochromes, cycling between $Fe^{2+}$ and $Fe^{3+}$.\n(c) Correct: iron activates catalase.\n(d) Correct: iron is essential for chlorophyll formation, so its deficiency causes chlorosis.\n(e) Incorrect: auxin synthesis needs zinc.\n(f) Incorrect: $Ca^{2+}$ uptake needs boron.\n(g) Incorrect: molybdenum is the component of nitrate reductase.\nSo 4 roles belong to iron.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-045","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":45,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): What appear to be symptoms of manganese toxicity may actually be deficiency symptoms of iron, magnesium and calcium.\nReason (R): Manganese competes with iron and magnesium for uptake and inhibits calcium translocation to the shoot apex.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT points out that manganese toxicity symptoms may really be induced deficiencies of Fe, Mg and Ca.\nR is true. Mn competes with Fe and Mg for uptake and blocks Ca movement to the shoot apex.\nR explains A, because these effects create the deficiencies that produce the observed symptoms.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-046","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":46,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements can best explain the term critical concentration of an essential element?","image":"","options":["essential element concentration below which plant growth is retarded.","essential element concentration below which plant growth becomes stunted.","essential element concentration above which plant remains in the vegetative phase.","none of the above"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT defines critical concentration as the concentration of an essential element below which plant growth is retarded.\nIf the level falls below this value, the element is said to be deficient.\nOption (2) says 'stunted', which describes a deficiency symptom, not the definition.\nOption (3) is unrelated to the concept.\nSo option (1) matches the NCERT definition exactly.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 12 (MCQ Q2)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-047","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":47,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are listed in NCERT as beneficial elements for higher plants?\nSodium, Silicon, Cobalt, Selenium, Nickel, Chlorine","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Beneficial elements are required by higher plants but are not counted among the essential elements.\nNCERT lists four of them: Na, Si, Co and Se.\nNi and Cl are not beneficial elements. They are essential micronutrients.\nSo 4 of the listed elements are beneficial elements.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-048","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":48,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, how many of the following are roles played by soil in plant nutrition and growth?\n(i) Supplies minerals formed by weathering and breakdown of rocks\n(ii) Harbours nitrogen-fixing bacteria and other microbes\n(iii) Holds water\n(iv) Supplies air to the roots\n(v) Acts as a matrix that stabilises the plant\n(vi) Supplies carbon to plants as carbon dioxide absorbed by roots, the main carbon source for photosynthesis","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: weathering and breakdown of rocks enrich the soil with dissolved ions and inorganic salts.\n(ii) Correct: soil harbours nitrogen-fixing bacteria and other microbes.\n(iii) and (iv) Correct: soil holds water and supplies air to the roots.\n(v) Correct: soil is a matrix that anchors and stabilises the plant.\n(vi) Incorrect: plants obtain carbon mainly as CO2 from the atmosphere, taken in through the leaves. It is not obtained from soil by the roots.\nSo 5 of the statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-049","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":49,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"In a typical set-up for nutrient solution culture, an aerating tube dips into the solution. Its function is to:","image":"","options":["add fresh mineral salts to the solution","remove carbon dioxide released by leaves","supply oxygen to the roots so they can respire","keep the solution warm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"In the culture set-up, the aerating tube bubbles air through the solution.\nThis supplies oxygen to the submerged roots for aerobic respiration. NCERT stresses that the solution must be adequately aerated for optimum growth.\nAdding water and nutrients is the job of the feeding funnel, not the aerating tube.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-050","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":50,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements about the fate of ammonia in plants is correct?","image":"","options":["At physiological pH ammonia exists as NH$_4^+$, which combines with $\\alpha$-ketoglutaric acid in reductive amination to form glutamate","In transamination, an amino group is transferred from a keto acid to an amino acid","Asparagine and glutamine are formed by removing an amino group from aspartic acid and glutamic acid respectively","Amides are transported mainly through the phloem because they contain less nitrogen than amino acids"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: NH$_3$ is protonated to NH$_4^+$. The reaction is $\\alpha$-ketoglutaric acid + NH$_4^+$ + NADPH → glutamate + H$_2$O + NADP, catalysed by glutamate dehydrogenase.\n(2) Wrong: the direction is reversed. The amino group moves from an amino acid to the keto group of a keto acid.\n(3) Wrong: these amides are formed by adding an amino group, not removing one.\n(4) Wrong: amides carry more nitrogen than amino acids and travel in the xylem.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-051","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":51,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Isolated root segments are placed in a solution of a mineral ion. In one set, a metabolic inhibitor is added that blocks ATP production. Compared with the untreated set, what is most likely to be observed in the treated set?","image":"","options":["Both the initial rapid uptake and the later slow uptake stop completely","The initial rapid uptake is blocked, but the later slow uptake continues normally","The initial rapid uptake into the apoplast still occurs, but the later slow uptake into the symplast is greatly reduced","Neither phase is affected, because all ion movement occurs through ion channels"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The first phase is passive movement into the free space (apoplast), mainly through ion channels. It does not depend on ATP, so it continues.\nThe second phase is entry into the symplast, which needs metabolic energy. Blocking ATP production greatly reduces it.\nOption (4) fails because only the passive phase relies on channels without energy.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-052","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":52,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"In the hydroponic system shown in NCERT (nutrient film technique), plants are grown in a tube or trough placed on a slight incline. Which statement about this system is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["A pump sends nutrient solution from a reservoir to the raised end of the tube","The solution flows down the tube and returns to the reservoir","The roots are continuously bathed in aerated nutrient solution","The solution rises from the reservoir to the raised end by gravity alone, without any pump"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: a pump circulates the solution from the reservoir to the elevated end.\n(2) Correct: the solution then flows down the incline and back to the reservoir.\n(3) Correct: the inset of the figure shows roots continuously bathed in aerated solution.\n(4) Incorrect: gravity only brings the solution down the slope. Raising it to the elevated end needs the pump.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-053","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":53,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Deficiency of either manganese or chlorine can reduce oxygen evolution during photosynthesis.\nReason (R): Both manganese and chlorine are required in the photolysis (splitting) of water.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Water splitting during photosynthesis releases oxygen.\nNCERT calls the splitting of water the best-defined function of manganese.\nNCERT also states that chlorine is essential for the water-splitting reaction that leads to oxygen evolution.\nSo a lack of either element impairs photolysis and lowers $O_2$ release. A is true.\nR is true and directly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-054","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":54,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Necrosis, or death of tissue (particularly leaf tissue), results from the deficiency of which of the following elements?","image":"","options":["Iron","Manganese","Zinc","Copper"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT states that necrosis is due to deficiency of Ca, Mg, Cu and K.\nCopper is therefore the correct choice.\nIron, manganese and zinc deficiencies cause chlorosis. They are not listed as causes of necrosis.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-055","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":55,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Plants can be grown in (Tick the incorrect option)","image":"","options":["soil with essential nutrients.","water with essential nutrients.","either water or soil with essential nutrients.","water or soil without essential nutrients."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Plants can be grown in soil that contains essential nutrients, so (1) is correct.\nThey can also be grown in water with nutrients added (hydroponics), so (2) is correct.\nHence either medium works when nutrients are present, so (3) is correct.\nWithout essential elements a plant cannot complete normal growth and its life cycle.\nSo growing plants in water or soil without essential nutrients is the incorrect option.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 12 (MCQ Q8)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-056","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":56,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In the first phase of mineral absorption by roots, ions enter the outer space or free space of cells (the apoplast) rapidly and passively.\nStatement II: In the second phase, ions are taken slowly into the inner space (the symplast), and this uptake requires metabolic energy.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. The initial rapid uptake into the free space (apoplast) is passive and needs no metabolic energy.\nStatement II is correct. Movement into the inner space (symplast) is slow and active, so it uses metabolic energy.\nNCERT describes ion absorption as exactly these two phases.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-057","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":57,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (element) with List-II (role in plants):\nList-I\nA. Calcium\nB. Molybdenum\nC. Potassium\nD. Copper\nList-II\nI. Component of nitrate reductase and nitrogenase\nII. Opening and closing of stomata\nIII. Formation of the middle lamella as calcium pectate\nIV. Associated with enzymes of redox reactions; reversibly oxidised\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-III: Calcium is used to synthesise the middle lamella of the cell wall in the form of calcium pectate.\nB-I: Molybdenum is a component of nitrate reductase and nitrogenase.\nC-II: Potassium maintains turgidity and controls stomatal opening and closing.\nD-IV: Copper is linked with enzymes of redox reactions and, like iron, is reversibly oxidised (Cu$^+$ to Cu$^{2+}$).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-058","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":58,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Rhizobium is a rod-shaped bacterium that is free-living in soil but fixes atmospheric nitrogen as a symbiont.\nStatement II: Frankia forms nitrogen-fixing nodules mainly on the roots of leguminous plants.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT describes Rhizobium as rod-shaped and free-living in soil. It fixes atmospheric nitrogen as a symbiont.\nStatement II is incorrect. Frankia nodulates non-leguminous plants such as Alnus, while legumes are nodulated by Rhizobium.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-059","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":59,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Reductive amination\n(B) Transamination\n(C) Glutamine\n(D) Ureides\nList-II\n(i) Formed from glutamic acid by adding an amino group\n(ii) α-ketoglutaric acid accepts ammonium\n(iii) Exported from soybean nodules\n(iv) Catalysed by transaminase","image":"","options":["A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(A) Reductive amination: $NH_4^+$ combines with α-ketoglutaric acid to give glutamate, so (ii).\n(B) Transamination: catalysed by transaminase, so (iv).\n(C) Glutamine: formed from glutamic acid by adding another amino group, so (i).\n(D) Ureides: exported from soybean nodules, so (iii).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-060","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":60,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Match the nitrogen-fixing organism in List-I with its description in List-II.\nList-I:\nA. Rhizobium\nB. Frankia\nC. Rhodospirillum\nD. Anabaena\nList-II:\n(i) Free-living anaerobic bacterium\n(ii) Free-living cyanobacterium\n(iii) Forms nitrogen-fixing nodules on roots of the non-leguminous plant Alnus\n(iv) Symbiotic bacterium in root nodules of legumes","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A. Rhizobium forms symbiotic nodules on legume roots such as pea and soybean: A-(iv).\nB. Frankia forms nodules on the roots of Alnus, which is not a legume: B-(iii).\nC. Rhodospirillum is a free-living anaerobic nitrogen fixer: C-(i).\nD. Anabaena, like Nostoc, is a free-living nitrogen-fixing cyanobacterium: D-(ii).\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-061","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":61,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Each option pairs a deficiency symptom with a group of elements whose deficiency causes it. Which pairing is incorrect?","image":"","options":["Necrosis (death of tissue) – Ca, Mg, Cu, K","Delay in flowering – N, S, Mo","Inhibition of cell division – N, K, S, Mo","Chlorosis (loss of chlorophyll) – Ca, Cu, B, Cl"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: NCERT lists Ca, Mg, Cu and K deficiency as causes of necrosis, especially of leaf tissue.\n(2) Correct: low N, S or Mo delays flowering.\n(3) Correct: low levels of N, K, S or Mo inhibit cell division.\n(4) Incorrect: chlorosis is caused by deficiency of N, K, Mg, S, Fe, Mn, Zn and Mo. Ca and Cu appear in the necrosis group, not the chlorosis group.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-062","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":62,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"A freshly cut, actively nitrogen-fixing root nodule of a pea plant looks pink inside. Which substance gives it this colour?","image":"","options":["Anthocyanin in the vacuoles of cortical cells","Nitrogenase enzyme","Leghaemoglobin","Phycoerythrin made by the bacteria"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Functional legume nodules contain leghaemoglobin, a pink pigment that acts as an oxygen scavenger.\nNitrogenase is the Mo-Fe enzyme that fixes nitrogen, but it does not give the nodule its pink colour.\nAnthocyanin is a vacuolar pigment of flowers and fruits. Phycoerythrin is an algal pigment.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-063","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":63,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"A plant growing in manganese-rich soil shows poor development at its shoot tip. Translocation of which element to the shoot apex is inhibited by excess manganese?","image":"","options":["Iron","Magnesium","Calcium","Boron"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT states that manganese inhibits the translocation of calcium to the shoot apex.\nThe growing tip is therefore deprived of calcium, even though calcium may be present in the soil.\nIron and magnesium are affected by competition for uptake, not by blocked translocation to the apex.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-064","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":64,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (deficiency symptom) with List-II (elements whose deficiency causes it):\nList-I\nA. Delayed flowering\nB. Inhibition of cell division\nC. Necrosis\nD. Chlorosis\nList-II\n(i) Ca, Mg, Cu, K\n(ii) N, S, Mo\n(iii) N, K, Mg, S, Fe, Mn, Zn, Mo\n(iv) N, K, S, Mo","image":"","options":["A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A: Low N, S and Mo delay flowering, so A matches (ii).\nB: Low N, K, S and Mo inhibit cell division, so B matches (iv).\nC: Deficiency of Ca, Mg, Cu and K causes necrosis, so C matches (i).\nD: Deficiency of N, K, Mg, S, Fe, Mn, Zn and Mo causes chlorosis, so D matches (iii).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-065","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":65,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"An element X is absorbed as a divalent cation. It activates both ribulose bisphosphate carboxylase-oxygenase and phosphoenolpyruvate carboxylase. Its deficiency symptoms appear first in older leaves. Which other role is also performed by X?","image":"","options":["Formation of the mitotic spindle during cell division","Maintenance of ribosome structure","Constituent of ferredoxin","Maintenance of cell turgidity and stomatal movement"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Step 1: Find X. It is a divalent cation that activates both RuBisCO and PEPcase, and it is mobile because symptoms appear first in older leaves. This identifies X as magnesium ($Mg^{2+}$).\nStep 2: Magnesium also forms part of the chlorophyll ring and maintains ribosome structure.\nStep 3: Why the others fail. Spindle formation is a role of calcium, also a divalent cation, but calcium is immobile. Ferredoxin contains sulphur. Turgidity and stomatal movement depend on potassium, a monovalent ion.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-066","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":66,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the fate of ammonia in plants are correct?\n(i) Ammonium ions are freely stored in plant cells because they are non-toxic even at high concentrations.\n(ii) Glutamine is formed from glutamic acid when the hydroxyl part of the acid is replaced by another $NH_2$ group.\n(iii) Asparagine and glutamine are structural parts of proteins.\n(iv) Soybean exports fixed nitrogen as ureides, which have a low nitrogen to carbon ratio.\n(v) In transamination, transaminase transfers an amino group from an amino acid to the keto group of a keto acid.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Incorrect: $NH_4^+$ is toxic at high concentration. It cannot accumulate and is quickly used to make amino acids.\n(ii) Correct: amides are formed by adding a second amino group in place of the -OH of the acid. Glutamic acid gives glutamine and aspartic acid gives asparagine.\n(iii) Correct: both amides are structural parts of proteins.\n(iv) Incorrect: ureides, like amides, have a particularly high N:C ratio, which suits xylem transport.\n(v) Correct: transaminase moves an $NH_2$ group from an amino acid to a keto acid. Glutamic acid is the main donor.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-067","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":67,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"In a legume root nodule, nitrogenase converts 4 molecules of $N_2$ completely into ammonia. According to NCERT, the enzyme needs 8 ATP for each $NH_3$ formed. Which option correctly gives the total ATP used and the metal composition of nitrogenase?","image":"","options":["64 ATP; Mo-Fe protein","32 ATP; Mo-Fe protein","64 ATP; Mg-Fe protein","32 ATP; Cu-Fe protein"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Step 1: Each $N_2$ is reduced to 2 $NH_3$. So 4 $N_2$ give 8 $NH_3$.\nStep 2: At 8 ATP per $NH_3$, the total is $8 \\times 8 = 64$ ATP. This equals 16 ATP per $N_2$.\nStep 3: Nitrogenase is a Mo-Fe protein (molybdenum and iron).\nThe value 32 ATP comes from wrongly charging 8 ATP per $N_2$ instead of per $NH_3$. Mg-Fe and Cu-Fe are not the correct metal composition.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-068","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":68,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Carbon, hydrogen and oxygen are classified as macronutrients of plants.\nReason (R): Carbon, hydrogen and oxygen are absorbed by plants mainly from the soil solution.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true: C, H and O are needed in large amounts, more than 10 mmole per kg of dry matter, so they are macronutrients.\nR is false: C, H and O are obtained mainly from CO$_2$ and H$_2$O, not from the soil.\nThe other macronutrients, such as N, P and K, are the ones absorbed from the soil as minerals.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-069","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":69,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following is a beneficial element required by some higher plants, rather than one of the essential micronutrients listed by NCERT?","image":"","options":["Nickel","Sodium","Chlorine","Molybdenum"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT lists eight essential micronutrients: iron, manganese, copper, molybdenum, zinc, boron, chlorine and nickel.\nBesides these, some higher plants need beneficial elements such as sodium, silicon, cobalt and selenium.\nSodium is therefore a beneficial element.\nNickel is a tempting choice because it was a late addition, but it is part of the essential micronutrient list.\nChlorine and molybdenum are also essential micronutrients.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-070","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":70,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Atmospheric nitrogen is abundant, yet plants cannot use it directly. The main reason is that:","image":"","options":["Nitrogen is present in the atmosphere only as nitrate","The two nitrogen atoms are joined by a very strong triple bond ($N \\equiv N$)","Nitrogen gas is highly toxic to plant cells","Atmospheric nitrogen is mainly present as ammonia"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Atmospheric nitrogen exists as the dinitrogen molecule $N_2$.\nIts two atoms are held by a very strong triple bond ($N \\equiv N$), so a large amount of energy is needed to break it.\nThis is why $N_2$ must first be fixed into ammonia or oxides before plants can use it.\nIt is not present in the air as nitrate or ammonia, and it is not toxic.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-071","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":71,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"In a hydroponic experiment, plants grown without element Y grow vegetatively but fail to set seeds. When element Y is still withheld but element Z is supplied instead, the plants grow normally and set seeds. Which conclusion about element Y is correct?","image":"","options":["Y is essential, because its absence prevented seed setting","Y fails the criterion of specificity, because its requirement could be met by another element","Y is a macronutrient, because its absence affected reproduction","Y fails only the criterion of direct involvement in metabolism"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"An element is essential only if it meets all three criteria: it is absolutely necessary, its requirement is specific and cannot be replaced, and it is directly involved in metabolism.\nHere Z fully replaced Y, so the requirement for Y is not specific.\nY therefore cannot be called essential, even though the first observation hinted at necessity.\nThe experiment gives no information about the quantity of Y needed (macro vs micro) or about its metabolic involvement.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-072","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":72,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Which group lists vegetables that NCERT cites as being produced commercially by hydroponics?","image":"","options":["Potato, carrot and radish","Tomato, seedless cucumber and lettuce","Wheat, rice and maize","Groundnut, sweet potato and beetroot"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT says hydroponics has been used successfully for the commercial production of tomato, seedless cucumber and lettuce.\nThe other groups are field crops or crops with underground storage organs. They are normally grown in soil and are not the examples given in the chapter.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-073","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":73,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Julius von Sachs demonstrated in 1860 that plants can be grown to maturity in a defined nutrient solution in the complete absence of soil.\nStatement II: Hydroponics has been used for the commercial production of vegetables such as tomato, seedless cucumber and lettuce.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. In 1860, the German botanist Julius von Sachs grew plants to maturity in a defined nutrient solution without any soil.\nStatement II is correct. NCERT names tomato, seedless cucumber and lettuce as vegetables produced commercially by hydroponics.\nBoth statements are true.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-074","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":74,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Ammonia produced by nitrogenase in root nodules does not remain as $NH_3$ in the cell. At physiological pH, it is converted into:","image":"","options":["Nitrite ions ($NO_2^-$)","Nitrate ions ($NO_3^-$)","Free nitrogen gas ($N_2$)","Ammonium ions ($NH_4^+$)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"At physiological pH, ammonia is protonated (it gains $H^+$) and forms the ammonium ion, $NH_4^+$.\nNitrite and nitrate are formed by nitrifying bacteria in the soil, not inside nodules.\n$NH_4^+$ is toxic to plants and cannot accumulate. It is quickly used to make amino acids.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-075","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":75,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is a free-living nitrogen-fixing bacterium that is anaerobic?","image":"","options":["Azotobacter","Beijernickia","Rhodospirillum","Frankia"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT groups free-living nitrogen fixers by their oxygen requirement. Azotobacter and Beijernickia are aerobic, and Rhodospirillum is anaerobic.\nFrankia is not free-living. It fixes nitrogen in a symbiotic association, forming nodules on the roots of Alnus.\nAzotobacter is the most tempting distractor because it is the best-known free-living fixer, but it is aerobic.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-076","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":76,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, the concentration of an essential element below which plant growth is retarded is called the:","image":"","options":["Deficiency range","Critical concentration","Toxic concentration","Optimum concentration"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT defines critical concentration as the concentration of an essential element below which plant growth is retarded.\nAn element present below this level is said to be deficient, so 'deficiency range' describes concentrations below the critical value, not the threshold itself.\nToxic concentration refers to excess, which reduces dry weight by about 10%.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-077","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":77,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following contribute nitrogen oxides to the atmosphere?\nLightning, ultraviolet radiation, industrial combustion, forest fires, automobile exhausts, power-generating stations, ammonification by decomposers, nitrate formation by Nitrobacter","image":"","options":["4","5","6","8"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT natural sources: lightning and UV radiation provide the energy to convert $N_2$ into nitrogen oxides (NO, $NO_2$, $N_2O$). That gives 2.\nHuman-linked sources: industrial combustion, forest fires, automobile exhausts and power-generating stations. That gives 4 more.\nAmmonification produces ammonia, not oxides, so it does not count.\nNitrobacter produces nitrate in the soil, not atmospheric oxides, so it does not count.\nTotal = 6.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-078","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":78,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the mechanism of mineral absorption is correct?","image":"","options":["Uptake of ions into the symplast is rapid and needs no metabolic energy.","Passive movement of ions into the apoplast usually occurs through ion channels, which are trans-membrane proteins acting as selective pores.","Both entry into and exit from the symplast are passive processes.","Much of our knowledge of ion absorption comes from studies on whole forest trees in the field."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: uptake into the symplast is slow and requires energy.\n(2) Correct: ion channels are selective trans-membrane pores that allow passive movement of ions.\n(3) Incorrect: both entry into and exit from the symplast need metabolic energy, so they are active processes.\n(4) Incorrect: most of this information came from studies on isolated cells, tissues or organs.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-079","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":79,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"A plant shows chlorosis, inhibited cell division and delayed flowering. The symptoms appear first in the young leaves, while the older leaves remain normal. Deficiency of which single element best explains all these observations?","image":"","options":["Nitrogen","Sulphur","Calcium","Potassium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Delayed flowering is caused by low N, S or Mo, which rules out Ca and K.\nInhibited cell division is caused by low N, K, S or Mo, and chlorosis by N, K, Mg, S, Fe, Mn, Zn or Mo. Both fit N and S.\nSymptoms first appear in young leaves, so the element must be immobile.\nNitrogen is mobile, so its symptoms would appear first in older leaves.\nSulphur is a structural, relatively immobile element and matches every observation.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-080","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":80,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"With regard to the Biological Nitrogen Fixation by Rhizobium in association with soybean, which one of the following statement/statements does not hold true.","image":"","options":["Nitrogenase may require oxygen for its functioning.","Nitrogenase is Mo-Fe protein","Leg-hemoglobin is a pink coloured pigment.","Nitrogenase helps to convert N$_2$ gas into two molecules of ammonia."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(1) False: nitrogenase is highly sensitive to oxygen and works only under anaerobic conditions.\n(2) True: nitrogenase is a Mo-Fe protein.\n(3) True: leg-haemoglobin is a pink pigment that scavenges oxygen inside the nodule.\n(4) True: nitrogenase reduces one N$_2$ molecule to two molecules of NH$_3$.\nSo the statement that does not hold true is (1).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 12 (MCQ Q6)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-081","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":81,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"A soil contains abundant decaying organic matter. A chemical is added that selectively inhibits Nitrobacter, while decomposers and Nitrosomonas remain fully active. Which nitrogen compound would tend to accumulate in this soil, and why?","image":"","options":["Nitrate, because its formation from ammonia is speeded up","Nitrite, because it is formed from ammonia but cannot be oxidised further to nitrate","Nitrogen gas, because denitrification takes over","Organic nitrogen, because ammonification stops"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Ammonification continues, so ammonia is still released from organic matter.\nNitrosomonas remains active, so ammonia is oxidised to nitrite.\nNitrobacter normally converts nitrite to nitrate. Once it is inhibited, nitrite builds up.\nNitrate formation actually falls, so option 1 is wrong.\nDenitrifiers need nitrate as their substrate, so option 3 is wrong.\nAmmonification is unaffected, so option 4 is wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-082","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":82,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements about biological nitrogen fixation is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Anabaena and Nostoc are cyanobacteria that fix nitrogen in the free-living state","Beijernickia is an aerobic, free-living nitrogen fixer","In legume nodules, the ATP used by nitrogenase is produced by photosynthesis of the bacteria inside the nodule","The ammonia formed in the nodule is protonated to $NH_4^+$ at physiological pH"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: NCERT lists Anabaena and Nostoc as free-living cyanobacterial nitrogen fixers.\n(2) Correct: Beijernickia, like Azotobacter, is aerobic and free-living.\n(3) Incorrect: the energy, 8 ATP per $NH_3$, comes from respiration of the host cells. Rhizobium in nodules is not photosynthetic.\n(4) Correct: at physiological pH, $NH_3$ is protonated to $NH_4^+$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-083","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":83,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following elements appear in NCERT's lists of causes of BOTH chlorosis and necrosis when deficient?\nNitrogen, Potassium, Magnesium, Calcium, Copper, Iron, Manganese","image":"","options":["One","Two","Three","Four"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Chlorosis list: N, K, Mg, S, Fe, Mn, Zn, Mo.\nNecrosis list: Ca, Mg, Cu, K.\nChecking each element: N, Fe and Mn cause chlorosis only. Ca and Cu cause necrosis only.\nK and Mg appear in both lists, so the count is two.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-084","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":84,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is a beneficial element for higher plants rather than an essential element?","image":"","options":["Boron","Silicon","Nickel","Chlorine"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT says that besides the 17 essential elements, some elements such as Na, Si, Co and Se are beneficial and needed only by certain plants.\nSilicon is therefore a beneficial element.\nBoron, nickel and chlorine are all essential micronutrients. Each meets the criteria of essentiality.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-085","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":85,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following symptoms is not due to manganese toxicity in plants?","image":"","options":["Calcium translocation in shoot apex is inhibited","Deficiency in both Iron and Nitrogen is induced","Appearance of brown spot surrounded by chlorotic veins","None of the above"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Manganese toxicity causes brown spots surrounded by chlorotic veins, so (3) is a true symptom.\nMn competes with Fe and Mg for uptake and with Mg for binding to enzymes.\nIt also inhibits calcium translocation to the shoot apex, so (1) is a true symptom.\nExcess Mn therefore induces deficiencies of Fe, Mg and Ca, but not nitrogen.\nThis makes statement (2) the one that is not due to Mn toxicity.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 12 (MCQ Q4)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-086","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":86,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following correctly pairs a micronutrient with the ionic form in which plants absorb it?","image":"","options":["Copper – $Cu^{+}$","Manganese – $Mn^{3+}$","Boron – $BO_3^{3-}$","Chlorine – $ClO_3^{-}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: copper is absorbed as cupric ions, $Cu^{2+}$. $Cu^+$ forms only during its redox cycling inside the plant.\n(2) Incorrect: manganese is absorbed as $Mn^{2+}$.\n(3) Correct: boron is absorbed as $BO_3^{3-}$ or $B_4O_7^{2-}$.\n(4) Incorrect: chlorine is absorbed as chloride ions, $Cl^-$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-087","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":87,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Calcium is required during the formation of the mitotic spindle and for the normal functioning of cell membranes.\nStatement II: Calcium is readily withdrawn from older leaves and moved to younger leaves, so it does not accumulate in older leaves.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Calcium is needed for forming the mitotic spindle, for normal membrane function and for activating certain enzymes.\nStatement II is incorrect. Calcium is a structural component that is relatively immobile, and NCERT notes that it accumulates in older leaves.\nBecause calcium is immobile, its deficiency symptoms appear first in young tissues.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-088","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":88,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following elements cause BOTH chlorosis and necrosis when deficient, as per NCERT?\nNitrogen, Potassium, Magnesium, Calcium, Copper, Iron, Sulphur, Manganese","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Chlorosis list: N, K, Mg, S, Fe, Mn, Zn, Mo.\nNecrosis list: Ca, Mg, Cu, K.\nElements common to both lists: K and Mg.\nN, Fe, S and Mn cause only chlorosis.\nCa and Cu cause only necrosis.\nSo 2 of the given elements cause both.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-089","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":89,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In plants, amides such as asparagine and glutamine are transported to other parts through xylem vessels.\nReason (R): Amides contain more nitrogen than the amino acids from which they are formed.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: amides are moved through the xylem to other plant parts.\nR is true: each amide carries an extra $-NH_2$ group, so it holds more nitrogen than the parent amino acid.\nNCERT links the two directly: amides are transported via xylem because they are nitrogen-rich. So R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-090","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":90,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the absorption and translocation of mineral ions in plants is incorrect?","image":"","options":["Entry of ions into the inner space (symplast) of cells is slow and requires expenditure of metabolic energy","The inward movement of ions into cells is called influx, and the outward movement is called efflux","Mineral salts are translocated mainly through phloem, carried by a descending stream of water driven by transpiration pull","Ion channels are transmembrane proteins that act as selective pores for passive movement of ions"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the second phase of uptake into the symplast is slow and active, so it needs metabolic energy.\n(2) Correct: NCERT calls ion movement flux. Inward movement is influx and outward movement is efflux.\n(4) Correct: passive uptake into the apoplast usually occurs through ion channels, which are selective transmembrane pores.\n(3) Incorrect: mineral salts move up through xylem with the ascending stream of water pulled by transpiration. Analysis of xylem sap shows that it contains mineral salts.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-091","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":91,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements about the fate of nitrogen in root nodules and plants:\n(i) Formation of two molecules of NH$_3$ from one N$_2$ by nitrogenase consumes 16 ATP.\n(ii) H$_2$ is released as a by-product of the nitrogenase reaction.\n(iii) At physiological pH, ammonia is protonated to NH$_4^+$, which is toxic to plants at high concentration.\n(iv) Amides such as glutamine and asparagine contain less nitrogen than the amino acids from which they are formed.\n(v) Nodules of soybean export fixed nitrogen in the form of ureides.\nHow many of the above statements are correct?","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: N$_2$ + 8e$^-$ + 8H$^+$ + 16ATP → 2NH$_3$ + H$_2$ + 16ADP + 16Pi.\n(ii) Correct: the same equation shows H$_2$ being released.\n(iii) Correct: ammonia exists as NH$_4^+$ at physiological pH and is toxic in excess, so it is quickly used to make amino acids.\n(iv) Incorrect: amides are formed by adding an extra amino group, so they contain more nitrogen.\n(v) Correct: soybean nodules export ureides.\nSo 4 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-092","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":92,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"An essential element has the following features:\n• It is absorbed as a divalent cation.\n• It activates enzymes of respiration and photosynthesis.\n• It is involved in the synthesis of DNA and RNA.\n• It helps maintain the structure of ribosomes.\n• Its deficiency symptoms appear first in older leaves.\nIdentify the element.","image":"","options":["Calcium","Potassium","Sulphur","Magnesium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Magnesium fits every clue. It is absorbed as $Mg^{2+}$, activates enzymes of respiration and photosynthesis, is involved in DNA and RNA synthesis and maintains ribosome structure.\nIt is mobile, so it moves from older to younger leaves. Its deficiency therefore shows first in older leaves.\nCalcium is also divalent, but it is immobile, so its deficiency appears first in young tissues.\nPotassium is monovalent, and sulphur is absorbed as an anion.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-093","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":93,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements about the fate of ammonia in plants is incorrect?","image":"","options":["Asparagine and glutamine are structural parts of plant proteins","Ureides exported by soybean nodules have a high nitrogen to carbon ratio","In transamination, the amino group of a keto acid is transferred to an amino acid","Most plants can assimilate both nitrate and ammonium ions"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: NCERT describes asparagine and glutamine as structural parts of proteins.\n(2) Correct: ureides carry fixed nitrogen with a particularly high N:C ratio.\n(3) Incorrect: the direction is reversed. Transamination moves the amino group from an amino acid to the keto group of a keto acid. A keto acid has no amino group to donate.\n(4) Correct: most plants can assimilate both forms, though $NH_4^+$ must be used quickly because it is toxic.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-094","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":94,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"The following events occur during nodule formation by Rhizobium on a legume root:\n(i) An infection thread carries the bacteria into the root cortex\n(ii) Root hairs curl\n(iii) Bacteria differentiate into specialised nitrogen-fixing cells (bacteroids)\n(iv) Rhizobia multiply and attach to epidermal and root hair cells\n(v) Nodule formation is initiated in the cortex of the root\nChoose the correct sequence.","image":"","options":["(ii) → (iv) → (i) → (v) → (iii)","(iv) → (ii) → (i) → (v) → (iii)","(iv) → (i) → (ii) → (iii) → (v)","(iv) → (ii) → (v) → (i) → (iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Rhizobia first multiply near the root and attach to epidermal and root hair cells (iv).\nThe root hairs then curl around the bacteria (ii).\nAn infection thread forms and carries the bacteria into the cortex (i).\nNodule formation is initiated in the cortex (v).\nBacteria are released from the thread into cells and differentiate into bacteroids (iii).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-095","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":95,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"In mineral nutrition, a mineral ion concentration in plant tissues is considered toxic when it reduces the dry weight of the tissues by about:","image":"","options":["1 per cent","10 per cent","25 per cent","50 per cent"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT defines the toxic concentration of a mineral ion as the tissue concentration that reduces the dry weight of the tissues by about 10 per cent.\nThis toxic level differs widely from one micronutrient to another.\nA 50 per cent fall is far beyond the defined threshold, and 1 per cent is too small to count as toxicity.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-096","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":96,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\nA. Ion channels\nB. Entry of ions into the symplast\nC. Efflux\nD. Use of radioisotopes of mineral elements\nList-II\n(i) Outward movement of ions from cells\n(ii) Evidence that minerals are transported through xylem\n(iii) Selective pores allowing passive movement\n(iv) Requires expenditure of metabolic energy","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-(iii): ion channels are trans-membrane proteins that act as selective pores for passive movement.\nB-(iv): entry of ions into the symplast is active and uses metabolic energy.\nC-(i): efflux is the outward movement of ions.\nD-(ii): tracing radioisotopes, along with analysis of xylem sap, supports transport of minerals through the xylem.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-097","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":97,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"In plants, ammonium ions are incorporated into an organic compound by reductive amination in the presence of NADPH and the enzyme glutamate dehydrogenase. Which compound accepts the $NH_4^+$ in this reaction to form glutamate?","image":"","options":["$\\alpha$-ketoglutaric acid","Pyruvic acid","Oxaloacetic acid","Phosphoenolpyruvic acid"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"In reductive amination, ammonium combines with a keto acid to form an amino acid.\nThe reaction is: $\\alpha$-ketoglutaric acid + $NH_4^+$ + NADPH → glutamate + $H_2O$ + $NADP^+$. Glutamate dehydrogenase catalyses it.\nPyruvic acid and oxaloacetic acid are keto acids, but they would give alanine and aspartate. They are not the acceptors that give glutamate.\nPhosphoenolpyruvic acid is the $CO_2$ acceptor in $C_4$ plants, not an $NH_4^+$ acceptor.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-098","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":98,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Arrange the following events of nodule formation in the correct sequence:\n(i) Root hair curls\n(ii) Rhizobia multiply and colonise the surroundings of the root\n(iii) Infection thread carries bacteria into the cortex\n(iv) Nodule establishes a direct vascular connection with the host\n(v) Bacteria attach to epidermal and root hair cells\n(vi) Inner cortical and pericycle cells divide","image":"","options":["(v) → (ii) → (i) → (iii) → (vi) → (iv)","(ii) → (v) → (i) → (iii) → (vi) → (iv)","(ii) → (i) → (v) → (iii) → (iv) → (vi)","(ii) → (v) → (iii) → (i) → (vi) → (iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"First, rhizobia multiply around the roots (ii).\nNext, they attach to epidermal and root hair cells (v).\nThe root hair then curls (i), and an infection thread carries the bacteria into the cortex (iii).\nInner cortical and pericycle cells divide to form the nodule (vi).\nFinally, the mature nodule develops vascular continuity with the root (iv).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-099","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":99,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (element) with List-II (role given in NCERT as an example of a functional category).\nList-I:\nA. Nitrogen\nB. Magnesium\nC. Zinc\nD. Potassium\nList-II:\ni. Activator of alcohol dehydrogenase\nii. Alters osmotic potential of cells\niii. Constituent of chlorophyll, an energy-related compound\niv. Structural element of biomolecules\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-iv, B-iii, C-i, D-ii","A-iii, B-iv, C-ii, D-i","A-iv, B-i, C-iii, D-ii","A-ii, B-iii, C-i, D-iv"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-iv: N, along with C, H and O, is a structural element of biomolecules.\nB-iii: Mg is part of chlorophyll, an energy-related compound.\nC-i: $Zn^{2+}$ activates alcohol dehydrogenase.\nD-ii: K alters the osmotic potential of cells, for example in stomatal movement.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-100","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":100,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"An element is classified as a macronutrient when it is generally present in plant tissues at a concentration of","image":"","options":["less than 10 mmole per kg of fresh weight","in excess of 10 mmole per kg of dry matter","less than 10 mmole per kg of dry matter","in excess of 100 mmole per kg of fresh weight"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Macronutrients are present in large amounts, in excess of 10 mmole $kg^{-1}$ of dry matter.\nMicronutrients (trace elements) are present at less than 10 mmole $kg^{-1}$ of dry matter.\nThe basis is dry matter, not fresh weight. Fresh weight varies with water content.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-101","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":101,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"During cell division, a newly forming cell wall uses an element as its pectate salt in the middle lamella. Which element is it?","image":"","options":["Magnesium","Potassium","Calcium","Iron"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Calcium is used to synthesise the cell wall during cell division, especially as calcium pectate in the middle lamella.\nMagnesium is the tempting wrong option. It forms part of chlorophyll and activates enzymes, but it does not form the pectate of the middle lamella.\nPotassium and iron have no structural role in the cell wall.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-102","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":102,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"A legume nodule develops normally up to bacteroid formation, but its tissues fail to become continuous with the vascular tissue of the root. Which outcome best follows from NCERT's description of nodule function?","image":"","options":["The infection thread would not form, so bacteria could never reach the cortex","Leghaemoglobin would turn the nodule green, because oxygen would no longer be removed","Nitrogenase would become independent of oxygen and fix nitrogen faster","Exchange of nutrients would be disrupted, so host-derived materials for energy would not arrive and fixed nitrogen products would not be exported"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The mature nodule establishes a direct vascular connection with the host for exchange of nutrients.\nThe high ATP demand of nitrogenase is met by respiration of the host cells, and fixed nitrogen (as amides or ureides) is exported to the plant.\nWithout vascular continuity, this two-way exchange fails, so option (4) fits.\nOption (1) is wrong because the infection thread forms earlier and has already acted. Options (2) and (3) misstate how leghaemoglobin and nitrogenase behave.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-103","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":103,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"The enzyme nitrogenase, which reduces atmospheric nitrogen to ammonia, is found exclusively in:","image":"","options":["Green algae","Prokaryotes","Leguminous plant cells","Fungi forming mycorrhiza"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that nitrogenase, the enzyme that reduces $N_2$ to ammonia, is present only in prokaryotes. Such organisms are called $N_2$-fixers.\nLeguminous plants cannot fix nitrogen themselves. Their root nodules fix $N_2$ only because they house the prokaryote Rhizobium.\nGreen algae and fungi are eukaryotes, so they lack nitrogenase.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-104","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":104,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the two reactions of nitrification:\n(1) $2NH_3 + 3O_2 \\rightarrow 2NO_2^- + 2H^+ + 2H_2O$\n(2) $2NO_2^- + O_2 \\rightarrow 2NO_3^-$\nWhich of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["In reaction (1), the oxidation state of nitrogen rises from $-3$ to $+3$","Reaction (2) is carried out by Nitrobacter, and nitrogen changes from $+3$ to $+5$","Reaction (1) releases $H^+$ and therefore tends to acidify the soil","Both reactions are reductions in which nitrogen gains electrons"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: N goes from $-3$ in $NH_3$ to $+3$ in $NO_2^-$.\n(2) Correct: Nitrobacter oxidises nitrite to nitrate, so N goes from $+3$ to $+5$.\n(3) Correct: reaction (1) produces $H^+$ ions, which lower the soil pH.\n(4) Incorrect: both steps consume $O_2$ and raise the oxidation state of N, so they are oxidations. Nitrogen loses electrons in both.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-105","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":105,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following elements first show their deficiency symptoms in the older (senescent) leaves of a plant?\nNitrogen, Potassium, Magnesium, Sulphur, Calcium","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Mobile elements are withdrawn from older leaves and sent to younger ones, so their deficiency shows first in older leaves.\nNitrogen: mobile, so older leaves are affected first.\nPotassium: mobile, so older leaves are affected first.\nMagnesium: mobile, so older leaves are affected first.\nSulphur: relatively immobile, so young leaves are affected first.\nCalcium: relatively immobile, so young leaves are affected first.\nThe count is 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-106","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":106,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Analysis of xylem sap shows mineral salts in it, and when radioisotopes of mineral elements are fed to roots, they are traced moving upward through the xylem.\nStatement II: Upward movement of minerals in the xylem is driven by ATP spent by living xylem vessel cells, independent of the water stream.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT cites both xylem sap analysis and radioisotope studies as evidence that minerals move through the xylem.\nStatement II is incorrect. Minerals move passively with the ascending water stream, which is pulled up by transpiration.\nMoreover, mature xylem vessels are dead and cannot spend ATP. So mineral movement depends on the water stream.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-107","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":107,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"A student wanted to test whether boron is essential for a plant. She made a 'boron-free' nutrient solution using ordinary tap water and laboratory-grade salts. The plants grew normally and completed their life cycle, so she concluded that boron is not essential. Why is her conclusion unreliable?","image":"","options":["Boron is a macronutrient, so it must be supplied in large amounts","Tap water and impure salts may contain traces of boron, which can meet a micronutrient requirement","Plants in hydroponics can fix boron from the atmosphere","Boron deficiency symptoms appear only in plants grown in soil"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Boron is a micronutrient, so it is needed only in very small amounts.\nTap water and salts that are not purified can contain traces of boron. These traces may be enough for normal growth, which hides the effect of leaving boron out.\nThis is exactly why solution-culture methods need purified water and salts.\nOption (1) is wrong because boron is not a macronutrient. Option (3) is wrong because plants cannot take boron from the air.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-108","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":108,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (process) with List-II (agent or description).\nList-I:\nA. Ammonification\nB. Oxidation of $NH_3$ to $NO_2^-$\nC. Oxidation of $NO_2^-$ to $NO_3^-$\nD. Denitrification\nList-II:\n(i) Nitrobacter\n(ii) Pseudomonas\n(iii) Breakdown of organic nitrogen of dead organisms to ammonia\n(iv) Nitrococcus","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-(iii): Ammonification is the decomposition of organic nitrogen to ammonia.\nB-(iv): $NH_3 \\rightarrow NO_2^-$ is done by Nitrosomonas/Nitrococcus.\nC-(i): $NO_2^- \\rightarrow NO_3^-$ is done by Nitrobacter.\nD-(ii): Denitrification is done by Pseudomonas (and Thiobacillus).\nOption 2 swaps the two nitrifiers, which is the common error.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-109","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":109,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is a free-living, anaerobic nitrogen-fixing microbe?","image":"","options":["Azotobacter","Beijernickia","Rhodospirillum","Rhizobium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT gives Rhodospirillum as the example of an anaerobic, free-living nitrogen fixer.\nAzotobacter and Beijernickia are also free-living, but they are aerobic.\nRhizobium fixes nitrogen as a symbiont in legume root nodules.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-110","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":110,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"A micronutrient is a component of both nitrogenase and nitrate reductase, two enzymes of nitrogen metabolism. Identify the element.","image":"","options":["Zinc","Copper","Molybdenum","Manganese"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Molybdenum is absorbed as molybdate ions.\nNCERT describes it as a component of several enzymes, including nitrogenase and nitrate reductase.\nBoth of these enzymes take part in nitrogen metabolism.\nZinc activates carboxylases, manganese activates many enzymes, and copper works in redox enzymes. None of these is a component of nitrogenase.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-111","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":111,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, when is an essential element said to be deficient in a plant?","image":"","options":["When its concentration in the tissue is just high enough to give maximum growth","When its concentration falls below the critical concentration","When its concentration reduces the dry weight of the tissue by about 10 per cent","When it is absent from the soil but present in the plant"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Critical concentration is the concentration of an essential element below which plant growth is retarded.\nAn element is called deficient when it is present below this critical concentration.\nA reduction of about 10% in dry weight is the NCERT criterion for mineral toxicity, not deficiency.\nThe level that supports maximum growth is the adequate range, not deficiency.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-112","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":112,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements about the concentration of essential elements in plant tissues is correct?","image":"","options":["Toxicity is any mineral ion concentration in tissues that reduces the dry weight of the tissue by about 50 percent","Macronutrients are generally present in plant tissues in amounts less than 10 mmole $kg^{-1}$ of dry matter","The critical concentration is the concentration of an essential element below which plant growth is retarded","Deficiency symptoms of relatively immobile elements appear first in the older leaves"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: NCERT defines toxicity as a concentration that reduces dry weight by about 10 percent, not 50 percent.\n(2) Incorrect: macronutrients occur in excess of 10 mmole $kg^{-1}$ of dry matter. Amounts below this describe micronutrients.\n(3) Correct: below the critical concentration, plant growth is retarded and the element is said to be deficient.\n(4) Incorrect: immobile elements cannot be moved out of old tissues, so their symptoms appear first in young leaves.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-113","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":113,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"In hydroponic experiments designed to find out which mineral elements are essential, purified water and purified mineral salts are used. The main reason is that:","image":"","options":["purified water dissolves mineral salts faster than ordinary water","impurities could supply unknown traces of elements and make the results unreliable","purified water prevents the growth of root nodules","plants absorb purified water by active transport only"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"These methods depend on knowing exactly which elements are present in the solution.\nOrdinary water or impure salts may carry trace amounts of elements, especially micronutrients. Such traces could hide the effect of leaving an element out.\nThe speed at which salts dissolve is not the issue.\nWater is not absorbed only by active transport.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-114","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":114,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"A plant is growing in soil containing a toxic excess of manganese. Which of the following effects would NOT be expected in this plant?","image":"","options":["Chlorosis linked to reduced iron uptake","Poor growth of the shoot tip due to reduced calcium reaching it","Reduced availability of magnesium for binding with enzymes","Rise in leaf magnesium and iron content as manganese displaces them from the soil into the root"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Expected: Mn competes with Fe for uptake, so induced Fe deficiency causes chlorosis.\n(2) Expected: Mn inhibits Ca translocation to the shoot apex.\n(3) Expected: Mn competes with Mg for binding with enzymes.\n(4) Not expected: competition lowers the uptake of Fe and Mg, so their content falls rather than rises.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-115","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":115,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Deficiency symptoms of calcium appear first in the young tissues of a plant.\nReason (R): Calcium is part of structural components of the cell, such as calcium pectate of the middle lamella, and is not easily released from mature organs.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Calcium is relatively immobile, so its deficiency shows first in young, growing tissues.\nR is true. Calcium is locked into structures such as calcium pectate in the middle lamella, so it cannot be withdrawn from older organs.\nR explains A: because calcium is not released from mature organs, young tissues cannot draw on it and are the first to suffer.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-116","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":116,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Soil nitrate is reduced back to nitrogen gas by the process of denitrification. This process is carried out by:","image":"","options":["Nitrosomonas and Nitrobacter","Azotobacter and Beijernickia","Pseudomonas and Thiobacillus","Anabaena and Nostoc"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT states that nitrate present in the soil is reduced to nitrogen by denitrification.\nThis process is carried out by Pseudomonas and Thiobacillus, which returns nitrogen to the atmosphere.\nNitrosomonas and Nitrobacter are nitrifiers. They oxidise nitrogen compounds and do not reduce them.\nAzotobacter, Beijernickia, Anabaena and Nostoc are free-living nitrogen fixers.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-117","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":117,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, how many of the following elements cause chlorosis when deficient?\nNitrogen, Calcium, Iron, Copper, Manganese, Molybdenum","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Chlorosis is the loss of chlorophyll, which makes leaves yellow.\nNCERT lists N, K, Mg, S, Fe, Mn, Zn and Mo as elements whose deficiency causes chlorosis.\nFrom the list, nitrogen, iron, manganese and molybdenum qualify, which makes 4.\nCalcium and copper deficiency are listed under necrosis (death of tissue), not chlorosis.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-118","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":118,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Carbon, hydrogen and oxygen are included among the macronutrients of plants.\nReason (R): Carbon, hydrogen and oxygen are mainly obtained from $CO_2$ and $H_2O$, while the other essential elements are absorbed from the soil.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true. C, H and O are present in large amounts, above 10 mmole $kg^{-1}$ dry matter, so they are macronutrients.\nR is true. These three come mainly from $CO_2$ and $H_2O$, not from soil.\nHowever, the macro/micro classification depends on the amount present in tissues, not on the source.\nSo R does not explain A.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-119","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":119,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Nitrogenase present in legume root nodules is a protein containing which two metals?","image":"","options":["Magnesium and iron","Copper and zinc","Molybdenum and iron","Manganese and molybdenum"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT describes nitrogenase as a Mo-Fe protein, that is, one containing molybdenum and iron.\nIt catalyses the conversion of atmospheric $N_2$ to ammonia.\nMagnesium is the central atom of chlorophyll, not a part of nitrogenase.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-120","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":120,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"During mineral absorption by root cells, the first phase is a rapid uptake of ions. Into which compartment does this occur, and of what nature is it?","image":"","options":["Into the symplast; active","Into the apoplast (free space); passive","Into the vacuole; active","Into the apoplast (free space); active"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Absorption of ions occurs in two phases.\nThe first phase is a rapid, initial uptake into the 'free space' or 'outer space' of cells, which is the apoplast. This phase is passive.\nThe second, slower phase moves ions into the 'inner space', the symplast, and needs metabolic energy.\nOption (4) is tempting, but uptake into free space does not need energy.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-121","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":121,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"More than 60 elements have been detected in plants. How many of them are considered essential for plant growth and metabolism?","image":"","options":["9","17","21","60"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Over 60 elements occur in plant tissues, but most are present only because the soil contains them.\nOnly 17 elements meet all the criteria of essentiality.\nThese are 9 macronutrients (C, H, O, N, P, K, S, Ca, Mg) and 8 micronutrients (Fe, Mn, Cu, Mo, Zn, B, Cl, Ni).\nThe option 9 is tempting, but it counts only the macronutrients.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-122","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":122,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Deficiency symptoms of calcium first appear in the young, developing parts of a plant.\nReason (R): Calcium is a structural component of the cell (e.g. calcium pectate of the middle lamella) and is not readily remobilised from mature organs.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Symptoms of relatively immobile elements such as calcium and sulphur appear first in young tissues.\nR is true. Calcium is locked into structural parts of the cell, so it is not moved out of older leaves.\nBecause old leaves cannot export it, young parts suffer first. R therefore correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-123","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":123,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements:\nI. Magnesium helps to maintain the structure of ribosomes.\nII. Nitrogen is absorbed by plants mainly as $NH_4^+$.\nIII. Calcium is needed during formation of the mitotic spindle.\nIV. Potassium is absorbed as $K^+$ and is involved in protein synthesis.\nWhich of the statements are correct?","image":"","options":["I, III and IV only","I and III only","II, III and IV only","I, II and III only"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"I. Correct: magnesium maintains ribosome structure, in addition to its role in chlorophyll.\nII. Incorrect: nitrogen is absorbed mainly as $NO_3^-$. Only some is taken up as $NO_2^-$ or $NH_4^+$.\nIII. Correct: calcium is required for formation of the mitotic spindle.\nIV. Correct: potassium enters as $K^+$ and takes part in protein synthesis.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-124","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":124,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Iron is absorbed by plants mainly in the form of ferrous ions ($Fe^{2+}$), and it is needed in smaller amounts than the other micronutrients.\nStatement II: Chloride ions, along with $Na^{+}$ and $K^{+}$, help determine the solute concentration and the anion-cation balance in cells.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect on two counts.\nIron is absorbed mainly as ferric ions, $Fe^{3+}$, not ferrous ions.\nIt is also required in larger amounts than any other micronutrient.\nStatement II is correct: $Cl^{-}$, with $Na^{+}$ and $K^{+}$, helps determine solute concentration and anion-cation balance.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-125","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":125,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"The first demonstration that plants could be grown to maturity in a defined nutrient solution, with no soil at all, was made by:","image":"","options":["Stephen Hales in 1727","Jan Ingenhousz in 1779","Julius von Sachs in 1860","Melvin Calvin in 1954"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT states that in 1860 the German botanist Julius von Sachs first showed that plants can be grown to maturity in a defined nutrient solution without soil.\nThis technique is called hydroponics.\nHales studied transpiration. Ingenhousz worked on the role of light in photosynthesis. Calvin worked out the C3 cycle. None of these is linked to soil-less culture.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-126","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":126,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Which mineral ion activates both ribulose bisphosphate carboxylase-oxygenase (RuBisCO) and phosphoenol pyruvate carboxylase (PEPcase), the two main carboxylating enzymes of photosynthetic carbon fixation?","image":"","options":["$Zn^{2+}$","$Mn^{2+}$","$Mg^{2+}$","$K^{+}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT gives $Mg^{2+}$ as an activator of both RuBisCO and PEPcase. This places magnesium in the enzyme activator category of essential elements.\n$Zn^{2+}$ is the tempting wrong choice. It activates carboxylases in general and alcohol dehydrogenase, but NCERT does not name it for these two enzymes.\n$K^+$ is mainly linked with osmotic potential and stomatal opening.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-127","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":127,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"In the nitrogen cycle, bacteria such as Pseudomonas and Thiobacillus are important because they:","image":"","options":["oxidise ammonia to nitrite in the soil","reduce nitrate in the soil to free nitrogen gas","convert organic nitrogen of dead remains into ammonia","fix atmospheric nitrogen in root nodules of legumes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Pseudomonas and Thiobacillus carry out denitrification. They reduce soil nitrate to $N_2$, which returns nitrogen to the atmosphere.\nOxidation of ammonia to nitrite is done by Nitrosomonas or Nitrococcus.\nConversion of organic nitrogen into ammonia is ammonification.\nNodule-based nitrogen fixation in legumes is done by Rhizobium.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-128","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":128,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"In the nitrogen cycle, ammonia in the soil is first oxidised to nitrite. Which bacteria carry out this step?","image":"","options":["Nitrobacter","Nitrosomonas and/or Nitrococcus","Pseudomonas and Thiobacillus","Rhizobium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: nitrification occurs in two steps.\nStep 1 is the oxidation of ammonia to nitrite, carried out by Nitrosomonas and/or Nitrococcus.\nNitrobacter is tempting but wrong because it carries out step 2, the oxidation of nitrite to nitrate.\nPseudomonas and Thiobacillus are denitrifiers. Rhizobium fixes $N_2$ in root nodules.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-129","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":129,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Hydroponics is the growing of plants in a nutrient solution without soil.\n(ii) Julius von Sachs, who first demonstrated soil-less culture, was a British botanist.\n(iii) Essential elements were identified by adding, removing or varying the concentration of an element in nutrient solutions.\n(iv) Deficiency symptoms of essential elements were discovered through such solution-culture experiments.\n(v) Hydroponic solutions need no aeration because roots receive enough oxygen from the shoot.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: this is the definition of hydroponics.\n(ii) Incorrect: von Sachs was a German botanist.\n(iii) Correct: elements were added, substituted, removed or varied in concentration.\n(iv) Correct: these experiments also revealed the deficiency symptoms of each element.\n(v) Incorrect: NCERT stresses adequate aeration of the solution.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-130","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":130,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Excess manganese in plant tissues competes with which two elements for uptake?","image":"","options":["Iron and magnesium","Calcium and potassium","Nitrogen and sulphur","Zinc and copper"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"According to NCERT, manganese competes with iron and magnesium for uptake.\nCalcium is affected in a different way. Manganese inhibits its translocation to the shoot apex, so calcium is not a competitor for uptake.\nThe other pairs are not mentioned in connection with manganese competition.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-131","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":131,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Atmospheric dinitrogen cannot be used directly by most living organisms. This is mainly because its two nitrogen atoms are joined by a","image":"","options":["single covalent bond that breaks too easily","double covalent bond","very strong triple covalent bond","weak ionic bond"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT represents dinitrogen as $N \\equiv N$. The two atoms are held by a very strong triple bond.\nBreaking this bond needs a large input of energy, so $N_2$ is chemically inert.\nAs a result, it must first be fixed into usable forms such as ammonia or nitrogen oxides.\nA double bond or an ionic bond is not the bonding found in $N_2$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-132","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":132,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the nitrogen cycle are correct?\n(i) During nitrification, nitrogen is oxidised from ammonia to nitrate.\n(ii) Ammonification is the conversion of soil nitrate into ammonia.\n(iii) Nitrate absorbed by plants is transported to leaves, where it is reduced to ammonia that finally forms the amine group of amino acids.\n(iv) All the ammonia formed by ammonification remains in the soil.\n(v) Denitrification by Pseudomonas returns nitrate nitrogen to the atmosphere as $N_2$.\n(vi) Nitrifying bacteria are chemoautotrophs.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: ammonia is oxidised to nitrite and then to nitrate.\n(ii) Incorrect: ammonification decomposes the organic nitrogen of dead plants and animals into ammonia. It does not act on nitrate.\n(iii) Correct: this is how NCERT describes nitrate assimilation.\n(iv) Incorrect: some of the ammonia volatilises, and the rest is converted to nitrate.\n(v) Correct: denitrification reduces nitrate to $N_2$.\n(vi) Correct: nitrifying bacteria get their energy by oxidising inorganic nitrogen compounds.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-133","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":133,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"In which ionic form do plants absorb iron from the soil?","image":"","options":["Ferrous ions, $Fe^{2+}$","Ferric ions, $Fe^{3+}$","Ferrate ions, $FeO_4^{2-}$","Elemental iron, $Fe^{0}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that plants obtain iron as ferric ions ($Fe^{3+}$).\nInside the plant, iron in carriers such as cytochromes switches reversibly between $Fe^{2+}$ and $Fe^{3+}$ during electron transfer.\n$Fe^{2+}$ is tempting because it appears in these redox changes, but it is not the form absorbed.\nFerrate ions and elemental iron are not absorbed by plants.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-134","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":134,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: An element that is needed only to reduce the toxic effect of another element, and plays no part in the plant's metabolism, does not qualify as essential.\nStatement II: An element in whose absence a plant cannot complete its life cycle or set seeds satisfies one of the criteria of essentiality.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. An essential element must be directly involved in metabolism. Merely offsetting another element's toxicity is an indirect role.\nStatement II is correct. An essential element must be absolutely necessary for normal growth and reproduction. Without it, plants fail to complete their life cycle or set seeds.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-135","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":135,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Inside the inner cortex of a developing legume nodule, Rhizobium cells released from the infection thread become modified into:","image":"","options":["Heterocysts","Rod-shaped bacteroids","Spherical endospores","Filamentous hyphae"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"After release from the infection thread, the bacteria become modified into rod-shaped bacteroids. These are the specialised nitrogen-fixing forms.\nHeterocysts are nitrogen-fixing cells of cyanobacteria such as Anabaena, not of Rhizobium.\nEndospores and hyphae have no role in nodule formation.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-136","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":136,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\nA. Mn and Mg\nB. Mn and Fe\nC. Mn and Ca\nD. Excess Mn by itself\nList-II\ni. Inhibition of translocation to the shoot apex\nii. Competition for uptake only\niii. Brown spots surrounded by chlorotic veins\niv. Competition for uptake as well as for binding with enzymes\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-iv, B-ii, C-i, D-iii","A-ii, B-iv, C-i, D-iii","A-iv, B-i, C-ii, D-iii","A-i, B-ii, C-iv, D-iii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-iv: Manganese competes with Mg both for uptake and for binding with enzymes.\nB-ii: Manganese competes with Fe only for uptake.\nC-i: Manganese inhibits the translocation of Ca to the shoot apex.\nD-iii: The visible symptom of excess Mn is brown spots surrounded by chlorotic veins.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-137","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":137,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"In a pot experiment, the control plants of a species have a mean tissue dry weight of 40 g. When a micronutrient X is supplied at increasing concentrations C1 < C2 < C3 < C4, the mean dry weights are 39.6 g, 38.8 g, 36.0 g and 30.0 g respectively. Using the NCERT criterion, which is the lowest concentration of X that should be regarded as toxic for this species?","image":"","options":["C1","C2","C3","C4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Criterion: a concentration is toxic if it reduces tissue dry weight by about 10 per cent.\nPercentage reductions from 40 g are: C1 = 1%, C2 = 3%, C3 = 10%, C4 = 25%.\nC3 is the first concentration to reach about 10 per cent, so it is the lowest toxic concentration.\nC4 is also toxic but is not the lowest such level. This value applies to this species only, because toxicity levels vary between plants.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-138","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":138,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A plant growing in soil with excess manganese may show symptoms of iron, magnesium and calcium deficiency.\nReason (R): Manganese is required by plants in larger amounts than any other micronutrient.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. Excess Mn competes with Fe and Mg for uptake and with Mg for binding to enzymes. It also inhibits translocation of Ca to the shoot apex. As a result, deficiency symptoms of Fe, Mg and Ca can appear.\nR is false. Iron, not manganese, is the micronutrient required in the largest amount.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-139","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":139,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"The decomposition of organic nitrogen present in dead plants and animals into ammonia is called:","image":"","options":["Nitrification","Ammonification","Denitrification","Nitrogen fixation"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT defines ammonification as the breakdown of organic nitrogen from dead plants and animals into ammonia.\nNitrification is wrong because it is the later oxidation of that ammonia to nitrite and then nitrate.\nDenitrification reduces nitrate to $N_2$.\nNitrogen fixation converts atmospheric $N_2$ to ammonia.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-140","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":140,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Fertilisers supply both macronutrients and micronutrients. Which one of the following is a micronutrient applied through fertilisers?","image":"","options":["Potassium","Sulphur","Phosphorus","Zinc"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT lists N, P, K and S as macronutrients supplied in fertilisers.\nIt lists Cu, Zn, Fe and Mn as micronutrients supplied in fertilisers.\nPotassium, sulphur and phosphorus are needed in larger amounts, so they are macronutrients.\nZinc is the only micronutrient among the options.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-141","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":141,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Nitrosomonas and Nitrobacter can grow without sunlight and without ready-made organic food.\nReason (R): Nitrifying bacteria are chemoautotrophs, which obtain energy by oxidising inorganic nitrogen compounds such as ammonia and nitrite.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT states that nitrifying bacteria are chemoautotrophs, so R is true.\nChemoautotrophs make their own food using energy released from oxidising inorganic substrates. They need neither light nor organic food, so A is true.\nR is the very reason A holds, because the energy for food synthesis comes from oxidising $NH_3$ or $NO_2^-$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-142","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":142,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Match the element with its associated functions/roles and choose the correct option among given below\nA. Boron — i. splitting of H$_2$O to liberate O$_2$ during photosynthesis\nB. Manganese — ii. needed for synthesis of auxins\nC. Molybdenum — iii. component of nitrogenase\nD. Zinc — iv. Pollen germination\nE. Iron — v. component of ferredoxin","image":"","options":["A-i, B-ii, C-iii, D-iv, E-v","A-iv, B-i, C-iii, D-ii, E-v","A-iii, B-ii, C-iv, D-v, E-i","A-ii, B-iii, C-v, D-i, E-iv"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A. Boron is needed for pollen germination, so A matches iv.\nB. Manganese is needed for the splitting of water to release O$_2$ in photosynthesis, so B matches i.\nC. Molybdenum is a component of nitrogenase, so C matches iii.\nD. Zinc is needed for auxin synthesis, so D matches ii.\nE. Iron is a component of ferredoxin, so E matches v.\nThis gives A-iv, B-i, C-iii, D-ii, E-v.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 12 (MCQ Q7)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-143","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":143,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"A micronutrient is a constituent of electron-carrying proteins such as ferredoxin and cytochromes. It also activates the enzyme catalase and is essential for chlorophyll formation. Identify the element.","image":"","options":["Copper","Iron","Manganese","Zinc"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Iron is a constituent of electron-transfer proteins such as ferredoxin and cytochromes.\nIt also activates catalase and is essential for chlorophyll formation.\nCopper also takes part in redox enzymes, but it is not linked with catalase activation or chlorophyll formation.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-144","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":144,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: For micronutrients, a moderate decrease in concentration causes deficiency symptoms, while a moderate increase causes toxicity.\nStatement II: Toxicity symptoms of micronutrients are distinct and therefore easy to identify.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Micronutrients are optimal only within a narrow range of concentration. A small fall causes deficiency, and a small rise causes toxicity.\nStatement II is incorrect. NCERT notes that toxicity symptoms are difficult to identify. For example, manganese toxicity may look like deficiency of other elements.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-145","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":145,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Which one micronutrient is a component of both nitrogenase and nitrate reductase?","image":"","options":["Molybdenum","Zinc","Manganese","Copper"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Molybdenum is absorbed as molybdate ions.\nIt is a component of several enzymes, including nitrogenase and nitrate reductase.\nBoth of these enzymes work in nitrogen metabolism.\nZn, Mn and Cu activate other enzymes but are not part of these two.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-146","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":146,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (part of a hydroponic set-up) with List-II (its role).\nList-I\nA. Aerating tube\nB. Feeding funnel\nC. Pump in nutrient film technique\nD. Slightly inclined trough\nList-II\n(i) Lets the solution flow back to the reservoir under gravity\n(ii) Adds water and nutrients to the culture\n(iii) Supplies oxygen to the roots\n(iv) Moves nutrient solution from the reservoir to the elevated end of the tube","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-(iii): the aerating tube bubbles air, so the roots get oxygen.\nB-(ii): the feeding funnel is used to add water and nutrients.\nC-(iv): the pump lifts solution from the reservoir to the raised end of the tube.\nD-(i): because the trough is tilted, the solution runs down past the roots and back to the reservoir by gravity.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-147","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":147,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"A macronutrient has the following features:\n• It is absorbed as a monovalent cation.\n• It is needed in abundance in buds, leaves and root tips.\n• It maintains the anion–cation balance and the turgidity of cells.\nIdentify the element.","image":"","options":["Calcium","Magnesium","Sodium","Potassium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Potassium is absorbed as $K^+$. It is required in large amounts in meristematic tissues, buds, leaves and root tips.\nIt maintains the anion–cation balance and cell turgidity. It also helps in protein synthesis and in the opening and closing of stomata.\nCalcium and magnesium are absorbed as divalent cations.\nSodium is not listed among the essential macronutrients.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-148","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":148,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For optimum growth in hydroponics, the nutrient solution must be adequately aerated.\nReason (R): Roots need oxygen for aerobic respiration, which provides the energy for active uptake of mineral ions.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that nutrient solutions must be adequately aerated to get optimum growth.\nR is true. Root cells respire aerobically, and much mineral uptake is active, so it needs ATP.\nWithout aeration, the roots run short of oxygen, respiration falls and ion uptake drops. R therefore explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-149","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":149,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about macronutrients is incorrect?","image":"","options":["$Mg^{2+}$ activates both RuBisCO and PEP carboxylase","Nitrogen is absorbed mainly as $NH_4^+$, and only small amounts are taken up as $NO_3^-$","Phosphorus is absorbed as $H_2PO_4^-$ or $HPO_4^{2-}$ ions","Calcium activates certain enzymes and helps regulate metabolic activities"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: $Mg^{2+}$ activates both ribulose bisphosphate carboxylase-oxygenase (RuBisCO) and phosphoenolpyruvate carboxylase (PEPcase).\n(2) Incorrect: the statement is reversed. Nitrogen is absorbed mainly as nitrate ($NO_3^-$), with some taken up as $NO_2^-$ or $NH_4^+$.\n(3) Correct: these are the absorbed forms of phosphorus.\n(4) Correct: calcium activates certain enzymes and plays an important role in regulating metabolic activities.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-150","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":150,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"During root nodule formation in a legume, which structure carries Rhizobium bacteria from the infected root hair into the cortex of the root?","image":"","options":["Infection thread","Plasmodesmata","Haustorium","Germ tube"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"After the root hair curls, the bacteria invade it. An infection thread then forms and carries the bacteria inward to the cortex, where nodule formation begins.\nPlasmodesmata are cytoplasmic links between neighbouring plant cells, not an invasion route for bacteria.\nHaustoria belong to parasites such as Cuscuta, and a germ tube grows from a spore.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-151","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":151,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Symptoms of nitrogen deficiency appear first in the older leaves of a plant.\nReason (R): Nitrogen is mobilised from senescing older tissues and moved to the younger, actively growing leaves.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Deficiency of mobile elements such as N, K and Mg shows up first in older leaves.\nR is true. During deficiency, these elements are withdrawn from senescing parts and sent to young leaves.\nThe older leaves are therefore depleted first, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-152","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":152,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about micronutrient toxicity are correct?\n(i) Micronutrients are optimum only within a narrow range of concentration.\n(ii) The toxic concentration is nearly the same for all micronutrients.\n(iii) The toxicity level of a given element is identical in all plant species.\n(iv) Excess of one element may inhibit the uptake of another element.","image":"","options":["One","Two","Three","Four"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: there is a narrow range between deficiency and toxicity.\n(ii) Incorrect: critical toxic concentrations vary widely among micronutrients.\n(iii) Incorrect: the toxicity level of an element differs between plants.\n(iv) Correct: for example, excess Mn hinders the uptake of Fe and Mg.\nTwo statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-153","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":153,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"In a dividing plant cell, the middle lamella of the newly forming cell wall is built mainly from a pectate salt. Which macronutrient forms this salt?","image":"","options":["Magnesium","Potassium","Calcium","Phosphorus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key fact: during cell division, calcium is used to build the cell wall, mainly as calcium pectate in the middle lamella.\nMagnesium is a tempting choice because it is also a divalent cation. However, its roles are in chlorophyll, ribosomes and enzyme activation, not in the middle lamella.\nPotassium and phosphorus do not form pectate salts in the wall.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-154","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":154,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"In a plant, the deficiency symptoms of an element appear first in the young, developing leaves. Which of the following elements is most likely to be deficient?","image":"","options":["Nitrogen","Potassium","Magnesium","Calcium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Symptoms appear first in young tissues when an element is relatively immobile and is not exported from mature organs.\nCalcium and sulphur are part of the structural components of the cell, so they are not easily released.\nN, K and Mg are actively mobilised to young tissues, so their symptoms appear first in older (senescent) leaves.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-155","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":155,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are roles of soil for plants, as described in NCERT?\n(i) Supplies minerals\n(ii) Harbours nitrogen-fixing bacteria and other microbes\n(iii) Holds water\n(iv) Supplies air to the roots\n(v) Acts as a matrix that stabilises the plant\n(vi) Serves as the main source of carbon for organic compounds","image":"","options":["Three","Four","Five","Six"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) to (v) are all correct. Soil supplies minerals and harbours N-fixing bacteria and other microbes.\nIt also holds water, supplies air to the roots and acts as a stabilising matrix.\n(vi) is incorrect. Plants obtain carbon from atmospheric CO2, not from soil.\nSo five statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-156","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":156,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Frankia produces nitrogen-fixing root nodules on which of the following plants?","image":"","options":["Garden pea","Alnus","Lentil","Alfalfa"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Frankia forms nitrogen-fixing nodules on the roots of non-leguminous plants. NCERT's example is Alnus.\nGarden pea, lentil and alfalfa are legumes. Their nodules are formed by Rhizobium, not Frankia.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-157","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":157,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (element) with List-II (form in which it is mainly absorbed by plants):\nList-I\nA. Phosphorus\nB. Iron\nC. Molybdenum\nD. Boron\nList-II\n(i) Ferric ions\n(ii) Molybdate ions\n(iii) $H_2PO_4^-$ or $HPO_4^{2-}$\n(iv) $BO_3^{3-}$ or $B_4O_7^{2-}$","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iv)","A-(iv), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iii)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A: Phosphorus is absorbed from soil as phosphate ions, $H_2PO_4^-$ or $HPO_4^{2-}$. So A-(iii).\nB: Iron is obtained mainly in the form of ferric ions ($Fe^{3+}$). So B-(i).\nC: Molybdenum is obtained as molybdate ions. So C-(ii).\nD: Boron is absorbed as $BO_3^{3-}$ or $B_4O_7^{2-}$. So D-(iv).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-158","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":158,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"In which ionic form(s) do plants absorb phosphorus from the soil?","image":"","options":["$PO_4^{3-}$ only","$H_2PO_4^-$ or $HPO_4^{2-}$","$P_2O_5$","$H_3PO_4$ molecules"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: NCERT states that phosphorus is absorbed from the soil as phosphate ions, either $H_2PO_4^-$ or $HPO_4^{2-}$.\n$PO_4^{3-}$ is also a phosphate ion, which makes it tempting, but NCERT does not list it as an absorbed form.\n$P_2O_5$ is only a way of expressing fertiliser content. $H_3PO_4$ is an un-ionised acid, not an absorbed ion.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-159","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":159,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Deficiency of which of the following elements is NOT listed by NCERT as a cause of chlorosis?","image":"","options":["Iron","Manganese","Calcium","Zinc"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Chlorosis is the loss of chlorophyll, which makes leaves turn yellow.\nNCERT lists N, K, Mg, S, Fe, Mn, Zn and Mo as the elements whose deficiency causes chlorosis.\nIron, manganese and zinc are all on this list.\nCalcium is not on the list. Its deficiency is listed under necrosis, along with Mg, Cu and K.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-160","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":160,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"A soybean crop inoculated with Rhizobium is grown in soil that is severely deficient in molybdenum but is regularly given nitrate fertiliser. What is the most likely outcome?","image":"","options":["Nodule nitrogen fixation fails, but nitrate assimilation stays normal, so no nitrogen deficiency appears","Both $N_2$ fixation in the nodules and reduction of nitrate are impaired, so the plants may show nitrogen-deficiency symptoms","Only photolysis of water is affected, so oxygen evolution stops while nitrogen metabolism stays normal","Leghaemoglobin synthesis stops, but nitrogenase keeps working normally"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Molybdenum is a component of nitrogenase, which fixes $N_2$ in the nodules.\nIt is also a component of nitrate reductase, which reduces absorbed nitrate.\nWithout Mo, both routes for obtaining usable nitrogen fail, so the plants may show nitrogen-deficiency symptoms such as chlorosis.\nOption 1 tempts but wrongly ignores nitrate reductase.\nWater photolysis needs Mn and Cl, not Mo.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-161","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":161,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Rhizobium lives as an aerobe in the free-living state, yet nitrogenase works inside the root nodule.\nReason (R): The nodule contains leghaemoglobin, which scavenges oxygen and protects the oxygen-sensitive nitrogenase.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Free-living rhizobia are aerobic, and nitrogenase does not operate in that state. During nitrogen fixation in the nodule, conditions become anaerobic.\nR is true. Nitrogenase is highly sensitive to molecular oxygen, and leghaemoglobin acts as an oxygen scavenger.\nThe oxygen scavenging by leghaemoglobin is exactly what allows nitrogenase to function in the nodule, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-162","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":162,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are free-living nitrogen-fixing prokaryotes?\nAzotobacter, Beijernickia, Rhodospirillum, Nostoc, Nitrobacter, Pseudomonas, Thiobacillus, Nitrosomonas","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Free-living N$_2$ fixers in the list:\n- Azotobacter and Beijernickia (aerobic)\n- Rhodospirillum (anaerobic)\n- Nostoc (a cyanobacterium)\nThat makes 4.\nThe rest do not fix N$_2$. Nitrobacter and Nitrosomonas are nitrifying bacteria. Pseudomonas and Thiobacillus carry out denitrification.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-163","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":163,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Biological nitrogen fixation is carried out only by prokaryotes.\nReason (R): The enzyme nitrogenase, which reduces atmospheric $N_2$ to ammonia, is found exclusively in prokaryotes.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Plants and other eukaryotes cannot fix $N_2$ on their own. Even in legume root nodules, the fixation is done by the bacterium Rhizobium, not by the plant.\nR is true. Nitrogenase is present only in prokaryotes, which is why such microbes are called $N_2$-fixers.\nWithout nitrogenase, $N_2$ cannot be converted to $NH_3$, so R directly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-164","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":164,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"A plant shows deficiency symptoms that appear first in its young, developing leaves. Deficiency of which element is most consistent with this pattern?","image":"","options":["Nitrogen","Calcium","Potassium","Magnesium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Symptoms appear first in young tissues when the element is relatively immobile and is not transported out of mature organs.\nCalcium and sulphur are structural components of the cell, so they are not easily released.\nN, K and Mg are actively mobilised to young tissues. Their deficiency therefore shows first in older (senescent) leaves.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-165","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":165,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following is a micronutrient for plants?","image":"","options":["Magnesium","Sulphur","Nickel","Calcium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Micronutrients (trace elements) are needed in very small amounts, less than 10 mmole per kg of dry matter.\nThey are Fe, Mn, Cu, Mo, Zn, B, Cl and Ni.\nMg, S and Ca are macronutrients, present in excess of 10 mmole per kg of dry matter.\nNickel is therefore the only micronutrient among the options.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-166","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":166,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (organism) with List-II (type of nitrogen fixer):\nList-I\nA. Azotobacter\nB. Rhodospirillum\nC. Nostoc\nD. Frankia\nList-II\n(i) Free-living cyanobacterium\n(ii) Symbiont in nodules of a non-legume\n(iii) Free-living aerobic bacterium\n(iv) Free-living anaerobic bacterium","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-(iii): Azotobacter is a free-living aerobic bacterium.\nB-(iv): Rhodospirillum is a free-living anaerobic bacterium.\nC-(i): Nostoc, like Anabaena, is a free-living cyanobacterium.\nD-(ii): Frankia forms nodules on Alnus, a non-legume.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-167","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":167,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"When glutamic acid is converted into glutamine in a plant cell, which structural change takes place?","image":"","options":["The amino group of glutamic acid is transferred to a keto acid","The hydroxyl part of an acid (–COOH) group is replaced by an –$NH_2$ group","An –$NH_2$ group is replaced by a keto (=O) group","A carboxyl group is removed as $CO_2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Amides form when another amino group is added to an amino acid.\nIn this addition, the –OH part of a carboxyl group is replaced by –$NH_2$. This turns –COOH into –$CONH_2$, so glutamic acid becomes glutamine.\nOption 1 describes transamination, which forms a new amino acid, not an amide.\nOption 3 describes loss of the amino group (deamination), and option 4 describes decarboxylation. Neither forms an amide.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-168","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":168,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (element) with List-II (role):\nList-I\nA. Nitrogen\nB. Phosphorus\nC. Sulphur\nD. Magnesium\nList-II\n(i) Constituent of the ring structure of chlorophyll\n(ii) Constituent of coenzyme A and ferredoxin\n(iii) Constituent of cell membranes; required for all phosphorylation reactions\n(iv) Required in greatest amount; constituent of proteins, nucleic acids and hormones","image":"","options":["A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A–(iv): Nitrogen is needed in the largest amount and is a constituent of proteins, nucleic acids, vitamins and hormones.\nB–(iii): Phosphorus is part of membranes and nucleotides, and is needed for all phosphorylation reactions.\nC–(ii): Sulphur is present in coenzyme A and ferredoxin.\nD–(i): Magnesium sits in the ring structure of chlorophyll.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-169","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":169,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about symbiotic nitrogen fixation is incorrect?","image":"","options":["Nitrogenase is a Mo-Fe protein that catalyses the conversion of atmospheric N$_2$ to ammonia","Leghaemoglobin acts as an oxygen scavenger and protects nitrogenase inside the nodule","Frankia produces nitrogen-fixing nodules on the roots of the leguminous plant Alnus","An infection thread carries the bacteria into the cortex of the root during nodule formation"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: nitrogenase is a Mo-Fe protein that reduces N$_2$ to NH$_3$.\n(2) Correct: leghaemoglobin, a pink pigment, scavenges O$_2$ and so protects nitrogenase.\n(3) Incorrect: Frankia does form nodules on Alnus, but Alnus is a non-leguminous plant.\n(4) Correct: an infection thread carries the rhizobia into the root cortex.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-170","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":170,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about identifying a mineral deficiency from plant symptoms is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Deficiency of a single element can produce several different symptoms","The same symptom may result from deficiency of any one of several different elements","Different plant species may respond differently to deficiency of the same element","Observing chlorosis alone in the leaves is sufficient to identify the deficient element"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: one deficiency can cause multiple symptoms.\n(2) Correct: the same symptom can arise from lack of different elements. For example, chlorosis is caused by N, K, Mg, S, Fe, Mn, Zn or Mo.\n(3) Correct: plants differ in how they respond to the same deficiency.\n(4) Incorrect: a single symptom is not enough. All symptoms in all plant parts must be studied and compared with standard tables.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-171","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":171,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The root nodules of leguminous plants contain a pink pigment called leghaemoglobin.\nReason (R): Nitrogenase is highly sensitive to molecular oxygen, and leghaemoglobin acts as an oxygen scavenger that protects it.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT notes that nodules appear pink because they contain leghaemoglobin.\nR is true. Nitrogenase works only under anaerobic conditions, and leghaemoglobin binds oxygen to protect it.\nThe pigment exists in the nodule precisely to keep oxygen away from nitrogenase. So R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-172","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":172,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Which enzyme catalyses the transfer of an amino group from glutamic acid to the keto group of a keto acid in plants?","image":"","options":["Glutamate dehydrogenase","Nitrogenase","Transaminase","Nitrate reductase"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Transamination moves the $-NH_2$ group from an amino acid, mainly glutamic acid, to the keto group of a keto acid.\nThe enzyme transaminase catalyses all such reactions.\nGlutamate dehydrogenase is the tempting wrong choice. It catalyses reductive amination of α-ketoglutaric acid, not amino-group transfer.\nNitrogenase fixes $N_2$, and nitrate reductase reduces nitrate.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-173","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":173,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Industrial combustion, forest fires, automobile exhausts and power-generating stations add which of the following to the nitrogen cycle?","image":"","options":["Nitrogen oxides in the atmosphere","Ammonia formed by decomposition of dead organic matter","Nitrite formed by chemoautotrophic soil bacteria","Free $N_2$ released from soil nitrate"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT lists lightning and ultraviolet radiation as natural agents that supply energy to convert $N_2$ into nitrogen oxides (NO, $NO_2$, $N_2O$).\nIt also names industrial combustion, forest fires, automobile exhausts and power-generating stations as further sources of atmospheric nitrogen oxides.\nAmmonia from dead organic matter comes from ammonification, nitrite comes from nitrifying bacteria, and $N_2$ comes from denitrification. None of these is produced by combustion.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-174","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":174,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Nitrosomonas oxidises ammonia to nitrite in the soil.\nStatement II: Nitrobacter reduces nitrate in the soil to free nitrogen gas.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is true. Nitrosomonas and Nitrococcus oxidise ammonia to nitrite, which is the first step of nitrification.\nStatement II is false. Nitrobacter oxidises nitrite to nitrate.\nThe reduction of nitrate to N$_2$ is denitrification, carried out by Pseudomonas and Thiobacillus.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-175","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":175,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Manganese activates many enzymes of photosynthesis, respiration and nitrogen metabolism. Which of its functions is described in NCERT as the best defined?","image":"","options":["Synthesis of auxin","Splitting of water to liberate oxygen during photosynthesis","Forming part of the enzyme nitrogenase","Promoting pollen germination"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The best-defined function of $Mn^{2+}$ is its role in splitting water, which releases oxygen during photosynthesis.\nAuxin synthesis needs Zn, nitrogenase contains Mo, and pollen germination needs B.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-176","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":176,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The range of concentration of an essential element below the critical concentration is termed deficient.\nStatement II: Any mineral ion concentration in tissues that reduces the dry weight of the tissues by about 50 per cent is considered toxic.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Critical concentration is the level below which plant growth is retarded, and concentrations below it are called deficient.\nStatement II is incorrect. NCERT defines a toxic concentration as one that reduces tissue dry weight by about 10 per cent, not 50 per cent.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-177","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":177,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Which element is a constituent of cell membranes, all nucleic acids and nucleotides, and is required for all phosphorylation reactions?","image":"","options":["Phosphorus","Nitrogen","Sulphur","Calcium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Phosphorus is a constituent of cell membranes (as phospholipids), certain proteins, all nucleic acids and nucleotides.\nIt is also needed for every phosphorylation reaction, for example in forming ATP.\nNitrogen is the tempting wrong option. It is present in nucleic acids, but it is not the element transferred in phosphorylation.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-178","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":178,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following micronutrients are essential for the water-splitting reaction that evolves oxygen in photosynthesis?\nManganese, Chlorine, Zinc, Molybdenum, Boron, Nickel","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Manganese: yes. Its best-defined function is splitting water to liberate $O_2$.\nChlorine: yes. $Cl^{-}$ is essential for the water-splitting reaction that leads to oxygen evolution.\nZinc: no. It acts in carboxylases and auxin synthesis.\nMolybdenum: no. It is part of nitrogenase and nitrate reductase.\nBoron: no. It acts in Ca uptake, pollen germination and similar processes.\nNickel: no such role is described.\nTotal = 2.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-179","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":179,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following plants form nitrogen-fixing root nodules in association with Rhizobium?\nAlfalfa, sweet clover, Alnus, lentil, wheat, garden pea, broad bean","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Rhizobium forms nodules on legumes.\nAlfalfa, sweet clover, lentil, garden pea and broad bean are legumes listed by NCERT, so they qualify. That makes 5.\nAlnus is nodulated by Frankia, not Rhizobium.\nWheat is a cereal that does not form Rhizobium nodules.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-180","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":180,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Maintenance of cell turgidity, opening and closing of stomata, and maintenance of anion–cation balance in cells are all roles of which element?","image":"","options":["Calcium","Sulphur","Nitrogen","Potassium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Key fact: potassium, absorbed as $K^+$, has all three roles listed. It keeps cells turgid, regulates the opening and closing of stomata, and balances anions and cations in cells.\nIt also takes part in protein synthesis and enzyme activation.\nCalcium affects membrane function but not stomatal movement or turgor in this way.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-181","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":181,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Arrange the following events of root nodule formation in a legume in the correct sequence:\nP. Root hair curls and bacteria invade it\nQ. Rhizobia multiply around the root and attach to epidermal and root hair cells\nR. Bacteria are released into cortical cells and differentiate into nitrogen-fixing cells\nS. An infection thread carries the bacteria into the cortex, where nodule formation begins\nT. The nodule establishes a direct vascular connection with the host","image":"","options":["P, Q, S, R, T","Q, P, S, R, T","Q, S, P, R, T","Q, P, R, S, T"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Q: Rhizobia first multiply around the root and attach to epidermal and root hair cells.\nP: The root hair then curls and the bacteria invade it.\nS: An infection thread carries the bacteria into the cortex, where nodule formation starts.\nR: The bacteria are released into cortical cells and form specialised nitrogen-fixing cells.\nT: Finally, the nodule makes a vascular connection with the host for nutrient exchange.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-182","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":182,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Chlorosis is the loss of chlorophyll that leads to yellowing of leaves. Deficiency of which one of the following elements is NOT listed by NCERT as a cause of chlorosis?","image":"","options":["Magnesium","Iron","Calcium","Zinc"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT lists eight elements whose deficiency causes chlorosis: N, K, Mg, S, Fe, Mn, Zn and Mo.\nMagnesium, iron and zinc are all on this list.\nCalcium deficiency is linked to necrosis, along with Mg, Cu and K. It is not linked to chlorosis.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MINERAL-NUTRITION-183","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mineral Nutrition","qno":183,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mineral Nutrition","type":"mcq","question":"Potassium plays an important role in the opening and closing of stomata. In which functional category of essential elements does this role place potassium?","image":"","options":["Structural component of biomolecules","Component of energy-related chemical compounds","Activator or inhibitor of enzymes","Element that alters the osmotic potential of a cell"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Stomatal movement depends on guard cells taking up or losing water.\nThis water movement is driven by changes in solute concentration, mainly $K^+$.\nPotassium is therefore NCERT's example of an element that alters the osmotic potential of a cell.\nIt is not a structural element like C, H, O and N.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 12 · Mineral Nutrition","difficulty":"easy"}]});
